/* THE FULLER TALE, SEASON BY SEASON — the secondary cast.

   js/wiki-x/seasons-principals.js covers the eight people whose pages are
   read most. This file extends the same hand-written, one-paragraph-per-
   season treatment to everyone else with real recurring presence in the
   show — not just point-of-view book characters, but anyone whose page was
   still falling back to the engine's auto-composed recap.

   Same merge contract as seasons-principals.js: it is merged into
   WIKI_EXTRA, not assigned, so it never clobbers a `paras`/`vs`/`fate` essay
   that already lives in js/wiki-x/ or js/wiki-extra.js for these people. It
   must load LAST among the wiki-x files, alongside (order between the two
   does not matter) seasons-principals.js.

   The prose is HTML-escaped on render, so every character here must be real
   Unicode — never entities. Every word is original to this site. A season
   the person is not in is simply left out; the engine skips it. */

(function () {
  var ADD = {

  "Eddard Stark": {
    1: ["Warden of the North, happier mending border disputes than sitting anywhere near a throne. He finds a litter of orphaned direwolf pups on the road and overrules his own man's order to kill them, letting each of his children keep one, and not long after beheads a deserter from the Night's Watch himself — a man who passes the sentence, he tells Bran, should swing the sword. He goes south to serve as Robert's Hand out of old friendship, and starts pulling at the thread of Jon Arryn's death until it unravels the true parentage of the boy meant to inherit the throne. He warns Cersei what he has found before he has the men to act on it, tries to hand the crown to Stannis instead of Joffrey, and is arrested in his own throne room for the attempt. He confesses to treason he did not commit, believing it buys his daughters safety and himself a place at the Wall — Joffrey has him beheaded on the sept steps anyway."],
  },

  "Catelyn Stark": {
    1: ["Lady of Winterfell, and the one who reads a room a beat before her husband does. Bran's fall puts her at his bedside praying to gods she was not raised with, and a stranger's dagger left for the killing finishes what the fall did not — she rides for King's Landing to warn Ned it was no accident. She recognizes the blade as Tyrion Lannister's on the strength of an old wager and takes him prisoner at a roadside inn, dragging him through the Vale to stand before her sister Lysa instead of a proper court. It costs Ned his last ally in the capital and starts a war she spends the rest of it trying to steer."],
    2: ["Sent to Renly's camp to buy her son an army instead of a rival crown, she watches him die in his own tent to a shadow with Stannis's face and no visible hand behind the knife. Brienne of Tarth, the only witness who does not flee, kneels and offers her a sword on the spot, and Catelyn takes it — the first oath sworn to her rather than to Robb. She returns to the war with no alliance to show for it, grieving a man she never much liked, and spends what's left of the season arguing for the peace her son keeps refusing to make."],
    3: ["She frees Jaime Lannister herself, over her son's objections, gambling that a hostage traded for her daughters is worth more than one kept in a tent, and sends Brienne to see him safely south. Robb's marriage to Talisa has already cost them the Frey swords they needed, and the wedding at the Twins meant to patch things over is a trap she reads half a verse too late — the music changes, the doors bar, and she is left with a knife at a hostage's throat and no one worth threatening with it. She kills Walder Frey's own wife with her bare hands, for whatever that is worth, before Black Walder opens her throat."],
  },

  "Robb Stark": {
    1: ["Eldest and heir, left to hold Winterfell while his father goes south and his siblings scatter — a boy playing lord until the news from King's Landing makes him one in earnest. He calls his father's banners the moment Ned is taken, and the army follows a nineteen-year-old south more on his family's name than his experience. At the Whispering Wood he proves he has a gift for it anyway, routing Jaime Lannister's host in the dark and taking the Kingslayer prisoner — a victory that wins him a crown of his own before it wins him his father back."],
    2: ["King in the North now, and undefeated in the field, which turns out not to be the same thing as winning the war. He falls for Talisa, a healer working his own camp's wounded, while his mother is away trying to buy him an alliance with Renly and his bannermen wait on the marriage to a Frey daughter that was promised to seal the crossing at the Twins. He keeps Jaime Lannister as a prize worth more alive than dead and keeps racking up battles that move the map without moving him any closer to King's Landing."],
    3: ["He marries Talisa, breaking the Frey match his mother negotiated to buy the army its crossing, and loses Karstark's swords soon after when he executes the old lord for murdering Lannister hostages in revenge. Weakened and short on allies, he agrees to mend the breach at Edmure's wedding to a Frey daughter, marching his depleted army and his pregnant wife into the Twins to make it right. The music turns, the doors are barred, and Robb dies watching his direwolf and then his wife and mother killed before him, cut down with his bannermen at a table where he came to make peace."],
  },

  "Tywin Lannister": {
    1: ["Lord of Casterly Rock and the mind actually running House Lannister, whatever the king believes about his own authority. He skins a deer in front of Jaime while explaining, without raising his voice, exactly how thin the family's reputation is and how much of it Jaime is spending. He sends the Mountain raiding along the Trident to answer Ned's justice with worse, and when Robb Stark's army routs his son's host at the Whispering Wood and takes Jaime prisoner, he takes personal command of the war himself, unbothered and unhurried about it."],
    2: ["He runs the war from Harrenhal and takes on a new cupbearer, a sharp-tongued orphan girl calling herself Nan, without ever suspecting she is Robb Stark's sister — and finds himself, against his own habits, half enjoying the conversation. He deals with the Mountain's men and the mess they leave behind the way he deals with everything, efficiently and without comment. When Stannis's fleet threatens King's Landing he marches the Lannister-Tyrell relief force in at the last possible hour of the Blackwater, and the city is saved on his timing rather than his son's."],
    3: ["Hand of the King again, and using the office the way he always meant to — arranging marriages like troop movements. He forces Tyrion to marry Sansa Stark and Cersei to accept a match with Loras Tyrell, brushing off both their furies as beneath the scale of what the family needs. Behind it he is quietly building the alliance that matters more than either wedding: a pact with Walder Frey and Roose Bolton to end the war in the North in a single night, at a wedding of the Starks' own."],
    4: ["He presides over Joffrey's wedding to Margaery Tyrell and, when the boy dies choking at his own feast, over the investigation that settles with practiced speed on Tyrion. He runs the trial like the family business it is, produces Shae to testify against her former lover, and sentences his own son to death without much visible struggle over it. Tyrion, cornered and out of allies, finds him in his own privy and levels a crossbow at him — Tywin's last words underestimate him, and then Tywin is dead in the chair, killed by his son."],
  },

  "Robert Baratheon": {
    1: ["King for seventeen years and visibly tired of it, happiest with a wine cup in his hand and a war story on his tongue. He rides north to make Ned his Hand after Jon Arryn dies suddenly, mostly because he trusts no one else in the capital, and the two of them spend a feast trading memories of a rebellion neither of them has stopped fighting in his head. He is a poor fit for peacetime — bored by councils, unfaithful to Cersei, generous with a kingdom he has mostly stopped paying attention to — and when he hears Daenerys Targaryen is pregnant he orders her killed, over Ned's objections, on the strength of an old grudge. A boar guts him on a hunt after too much wine, poured by a squire doing the queen's bidding, and he dies naming Ned protector of the realm in a will Cersei burns before anyone can read it aloud."],
  },

  "Stannis Baratheon": {
    2: ["Robert's younger brother and, by his own reading of the law, the rightful king now that Joffrey is a bastard by another name. He rejects the Seven for the Lord of Light at Melisandre's word, burns the old statues on the beach at Dragonstone, and lets her use him to birth something in a cave that walks into Renly's tent and kills him without a mark to show for it. He sails on King's Landing with the larger fleet and loses the city anyway, to wildfire in the bay and a Tyrell army arriving behind him — the battle ends with his ships burning and his ambitions back where they started, alone on Dragonstone."],
    3: ["Diminished after the Blackwater and short on men who still believe the fire was a sign rather than a defeat. He lets Melisandre go looking for king's blood elsewhere and locks up Davos for trying to stop Gendry from being bled for it, though it's Davos who teaches himself to read in that cell and Davos he pardons when letters from the Wall about walkers in the snow finally convince him the war worth fighting is not the one against the Lannisters."],
    4: ["Broke and short on swords, he sends Davos to the Iron Bank in Braavos and comes back with just enough coin and just enough of a fleet to matter again. He marches what's left of his army to the Wall, arrives at Castle Black in the middle of Mance Rayder's assault on the gate, and breaks the wildling host from behind with cavalry the Night's Watch never could have raised on its own. It buys him men, a foothold in the North, and a debt from Jon Snow he intends to collect."],
    5: ["At the Wall through the worst of the winter, trading with Jon Snow for supplies and swearing he means to take the North for the family rather than for himself. He has Mance Rayder burned for refusing to kneel, and it is Jon's arrow that ends it mercifully instead. He marches on Winterfell against Davos's counsel, loses men and horses to snow and desertion, and lets Melisandre burn his own daughter Shireen alive to turn the wind — it does not save the campaign. His army broken outside Winterfell's walls, he is found alone in the trees and killed by Brienne of Tarth, who has waited a long time to collect on Renly's death."],
  },

  "Theon Greyjoy": {
    1: ["Ward of Winterfell since he was a boy, taken as a hostage after his father's rebellion and raised half a Stark without ever quite becoming one. He is glib, restless, and a little too pleased with himself, forever needling Jon Snow about his bastardy to cover for his own uncertain place in the household. He follows Robb south when the war starts, more sword than statesman, still working out which family he actually belongs to."],
    2: ["Sent home to the Iron Islands to bring his father's fleet to Robb's cause, and finds only scorn — Balon wants no part of the war and no use for the son who left. Stinging from the rejection, Theon takes matters into his own hands and seizes Winterfell with a handful of ironborn while its garrison is away, a hollow conquest he cannot hold and does not know how to relinquish gracefully. He murders two miller's boys and passes their burned bodies off as Bran and Rickon to save face, then loses the castle anyway when Ramsay Snow arrives promising rescue."],
    3: ["The rescue is a lie, and Ramsay Snow takes him apart for it, over long months in the Dreadfort's cellars that Theon experiences less as punishment than as a game whose rules keep changing — a door left open, a horse saddled and ready, freedom offered and then snatched back at the last possible moment, again and again, until he stops believing escape is a real thing that happens to real people. By the time the games stop he answers only to the name Reek, flinches from kindness as readily as cruelty, and has all but forgotten there was ever a Theon Greyjoy to go back to."],
    4: ["Ramsay parades his creation like a hunting dog he trained himself, using him to talk the ironborn garrison at Moat Cailin into surrendering without a fight, then dressing him up as Theon Greyjoy once more to walk Sansa Stark down the aisle at Ramsay's own wedding — a performance of the boy he used to be, delivered by someone who no longer has any idea how to get back to being him. It is the closest thing to recognition Reek gets all year, and he flinches from it as if it were another order."],
    5: ["Kept at heel in Winterfell through Ramsay's marriage and the cruelties that come with it, flinching at every order and offering none of the resistance the old Theon would have, until Sansa — trapped in the same house, with fewer options than he has — starts asking more of him than fear alone has left him able to give. When the moment finally comes he chooses her over the only safety he's known in years, and the two of them go off the castle wall together into the snow below rather than stay another night under Ramsay's roof."],
    6: ["On the run north with Sansa through open country and worse weather, delivering her safely to Jon at Castle Black rather than peeling off toward any safety of his own — the debt from Winterfell finally paid in full. With that settled he sails home to the Iron Islands for the first time in years, arriving just as a kingsmoot convenes to choose a new ruler after their father's death. He has as strong a claim as anyone in the hall, and uses what's left of it to back his sister Yara instead of taking the crown for himself."],
    7: ["Yara's fleet is ambushed by their uncle Euron at sea, and Theon — the old panic taking over before he can stop it — abandons the fight rather than go down defending her ship, an old failure finding a fresh way to repeat itself. Yara is taken captive, and Theon spends the rest of the year at Dragonstone, useless and ashamed, trying to talk himself into being the man who goes back for her instead of the one who left her to Euron in the first place."],
    8: ["He does go back, leading ironborn fighters to free Yara from Euron's camp, then returns to Winterfell to make his peace with Sansa and stand for the family that once called him ward, hostage, and finally something like a son. In the battle for the living he holds the godswood at Bran's side against the dead until there is nothing left to hold it with."],
  },

  "Sandor Clegane": {
    1: ["Joffrey's sworn shield and the fire-scarred terror of every hall he walks into, though the fear he inspires sits oddly next to how plainly he despises the boy he serves. He runs down Arya's friend Mycah on Joffrey's order without a flicker of hesitation, and later, ordered into the melee against his own brother Gregor, is pulled off only when Ned Stark calls a halt — the closest thing to protection Sansa Stark has in that court, offered in a growl rather than a courtesy."],
    2: ["Stands over Sansa more than once when the mob or the court would otherwise have her, protection dressed up as indifference. At the height of the Battle of the Blackwater, with wildfire turning the bay green and his men breaking around him, something in him breaks too — he walks off the field and out of the Kingsguard, offering to take Sansa with him. She refuses, and he goes alone."],
    3: ["Caught by Arya Stark's makeshift company not long after deserting, then handed over to the Brotherhood Without Banners, who try him for his crimes and let a trial by fire decide it — he beats Beric Dondarrion in single combat and walks free, dragging Arya along as a hostage worth ransoming. The road turns them into something neither expected, closer to companions than captor and captive, right up until the Twins, where the slaughter of the Red Wedding reaches them before they do."],
    4: ["Still hauling Arya toward whatever relative will pay for her, the two of them living hand to mouth on a country gone lawless — he kills a man twice his size without much ceremony and takes his boots. At the Vale's gate he runs into Brienne of Tarth, sworn to find Sansa's sisters, and the two fight over which of them has the better claim to Arya; he loses, badly, and is left for dead on a hillside while she walks away without him."],
    6: ["Alive after all — the axe blow that should have finished him only leaves a body to be found by a wandering septon running a quiet farming community, and for a while Sandor lets himself believe there might be a version of his life that doesn't end in someone's blood. It doesn't last. The men who slaughter that community while he's away burying a corpse take the fantasy with them, and something in him that had gone quiet for a season wakes back up — he picks up an axe, falls in with the Brotherhood Without Banners, and starts hunting the killers down one by one, without much interest in taking them alive."],
    7: ["At Dragonstone, staring into Melisandre's flames at Daenerys's court, he sees something in the fire he cannot explain and doesn't want to, and delivers the message to Jon anyway, gruffly, because the alternative is pretending he saw nothing at all. Not long after, he travels north beyond the Wall with Jon's party to snatch a wight for proof, an expedition that tests every scrap of self-preservation he has left and puts him, of all people, on speaking terms with a giant."],
    8: ["Fights through the long night at Winterfell and comes out the other side with no further use for surviving it than settling one thing — his brother. He walks south into King's Landing as the city burns around him, past armies and dragonfire alike, for a reckoning with Gregor that has been owed since before either of them can properly remember, and the two of them go down together into the fire."],
  },

  "Brienne of Tarth": {
    2: ["Wins Renly Baratheon's melee tournament outright and asks, as her prize, for a place in his Kingsguard rather than any softer reward — an unheard-of request he grants anyway, charmed by the nerve of it. She is at his side, devoted past reason, when a shadow with Stannis's face kills him in his own tent, and flees the scene covered in his blood with no one likely to believe how he actually died. Catelyn Stark, who saw enough to know Brienne didn't do it, takes her into service instead."],
    3: ["Sworn to Catelyn's cause, she is given an unwelcome charge — escort Jaime Lannister safely to King's Landing in exchange for the Stark girls, a prisoner swap she has no love for and every intention of honoring anyway. Locke's men take them both captive, and when Brienne is thrown into a pit to fight a bear for entertainment, Jaime — one hand gone, debt still unpaid — climbs down after her rather than watch."],
    4: ["Released from Jaime's service with his father's Valyrian steel sword and a new charge: find Sansa and Arya Stark and keep them safe, whatever that costs. Squire Podrick Payne in tow, she tracks Arya as far as the Vale, only to be mistaken for an enemy by the Hound and forced to fight him for her at the Bloody Gate — a brutal, close thing that she wins, and Arya still won't go with her."],
    5: ["Waits outside Winterfell through the winter, watching for a candle Sansa was told to light in a high window if she ever needed rescuing, certain it will come and increasingly unwilling to leave without it. When Stannis Baratheon's broken army stumbles past her position after its defeat, she finally gets to settle an old debt from Renly's death, sword in hand."],
    6: ["Finds Sansa fleeing Winterfell with Theon before any candle is ever lit, and the search that has occupied her for years simply ends, replaced by a sworn place at Sansa's side. Sent to Riverrun to talk the Blackfish into surrendering the castle to save the Stark forces marching for Winterfell, she instead runs into Jaime commanding the siege from the other side — an old debt now standing on opposite ends of the same battlefield."],
    7: ["Stationed at Winterfell as its castellan in all but name, training Podrick and keeping an uneasy peace between Sansa and a newly returned Arya, whose skills leave Brienne visibly rattled the one time they cross swords in practice. When Jaime arrives with the rest of the great households summoned south, she goes too, standing guard over Sansa at the parley in King's Landing's dragonpit while old enemies try, unconvincingly, to be allies."],
    8: ["Knighted by Jaime before the Battle of Winterfell — the honor she wanted at Renly's tourney years ago, finally given, by the person least expected to give it. She survives the night at his side, and the fragile thing between them survives it too, right up until he chooses to ride south for Cersei anyway. She stays behind to write the true account of his life into the White Book, since no one else will."],
  },

  "Jorah Mormont": {
    1: ["An exiled knight who sold poachers into slavery to pay his debts and has been running from that shame ever since, now serving as advisor and translator at Daenerys Targaryen's side in her brother Viserys's court-in-exile among the Dothraki. He watches her married off to Khal Drogo, watches her grow into something Viserys never will, and quietly falls in love with her long before he says so out loud, if he ever fully does. When Drogo dies and the tribe scatters, he is one of the few who stays for the fire that follows."],
    2: ["Guides Daenerys and her small, starving khalasar across the Red Waste to Qarth, where hospitality curdles fast — Xaro Xhoan Daxos's promises turn out to be worth less than his palace, and her dragons are stolen out from under her. Jorah goes with her into the House of the Undying to get them back, and is offered a vision inside it of the life he might have had, which he leaves behind without much hesitation."],
    3: ["At her side through the long Slaver's Bay campaign — Astapor bought with fire and blood, Yunkai talked into surrender, an army of Unsullied purchased and then freed and then followed anyway — his counsel as steady as it's been since the Dothraki grass, even as a new arrival starts to complicate things. The sellsword captain Daario Naharis joins her cause with more charm than sense, and begins drawing Daenerys's eye in a way that years of Jorah's quiet devotion never quite have, a shift neither of them says out loud yet."],
    4: ["Ruling Meereen at her side, useful and unloved in equal measure, until Barristan Selmy produces an old royal pardon in Jorah's own hand — proof he once spied on her for the Iron Throne in her earliest days in Essos, before Barristan himself even entered her service. She banishes him on the spot, refusing to hear excuses about a debt paid or a loyalty since made real, and he rides out of the city he helped her take with nowhere obvious to go."],
    5: ["Wandering exile agrees with him badly. He captures Tyrion Lannister in the ruins of Old Valyria hoping to buy his way back into Daenerys's graces, is sold into slavery for his trouble, and ends up fighting in Meereen's reopened fighting pits — where Daenerys finally sees him again, right before she also sees the greyscale spreading up his arm and orders him gone a second time."],
    6: ["Banished but not gone, he and Daario Naharis track Daenerys across the Dothraki sea after Khal Moro's riders carry her off, finding her just as she talks her way into leading every khalasar in Vaes Dothrak rather than dying in one of their tents. With that crisis past, Jorah finally says the thing he's been swallowing for years and shows her the greyscale creeping up his arm to prove he means it. She sends him away again, gentler this time, with an order rather than a banishment — find a cure, and come back to her as something other than the spy he started out as."],
    7: ["Finds his way to the Citadel in Oldtown, where an archmaester pronounces the greyscale untreatable and recommends he go quietly die of it somewhere useful. Samwell Tarly, newly arrived and appalled by how little the maesters are willing to try, performs the brutal old cure himself against direct orders, and it works. Cured, Jorah sails for Dragonstone, rejoins Daenerys's council exactly as promised, and volunteers for the mission beyond the Wall to capture a wight — wounded on the ice for his trouble, but present when the dragon arrives to pull the survivors out."],
    8: ["Stands with Daenerys through the long night at Winterfell, sword drawn against the dead in the godswood and everywhere else the line breaks, the same devotion he's carried since a khalasar in the grass years before. He falls defending her when the battle turns worst, the debt from Meereen and everything before it finally called due in full."],
  },

  "Tormund Giantsbane": {
    3: ["A wildling raider under Mance Rayder's banner, loud, thoroughly pleased with his own reputation, and part of the war party that takes Jon Snow prisoner north of the Wall rather than killing him outright. He's there when Jon's loyalty to the Free Folk is tested in blood, and there again leading the climb up the sheer icy face of the Wall itself with Ygritte close behind him, more amused than suspicious about the crow who claims to have turned his cloak for good — he has his doubts about Jon Snow long before anyone else does, and mostly keeps them to himself."],
    4: ["Leads the wildling assault on Castle Black from the ground while Ygritte's raiders come at it from above, throwing everything the Free Folk have at a fortress garrisoned by a fraction of the men it would take to hold it properly against a force this size. The attack breaks against the Night's Watch anyway, costly on both sides and decisive on neither, the wall itself and the men holding it proving harder to crack than the war council expected. Tormund survives the rout that follows, one of the few senior raiders left standing, and fights on under a King-Beyond-the-Wall who does not."],
    5: ["Brought south under guard to Castle Black to serve as go-between when Jon proposes an alliance the rest of the Night's Watch wants no part of. At Hardhome he helps evacuate as many of the Free Folk as the ships can carry while the dead close in from the treeline, and gets Jon off the beach alive when the retreat turns into a rout."],
    6: ["Marches a wildling army south to Winterfell on Jon Snow's word alone, trusting a promise made by a crow who by rights should still be an enemy, and asking little in return beyond a place for his people to live. It is that army, arriving late and nearly too late, that turns the Battle of the Bastards from a slaughter into a victory — Rickon already dead and the field all but lost when Tormund's horn finally sounds over the ridge and the tide of the fight turns with it."],
    7: ["Joins the expedition beyond the Wall to capture a wight for the southern lords, trading barbed affection with the Hound the whole miserable trek and making no secret of his admiration for Brienne of Tarth back at Winterfell. He survives the ice lake and the retreat to Eastwatch, one of a shrinking handful who make it out of that whole doomed errand."],
    8: ["Fights through the Battle of Winterfell and lives to see the dawn after it, one of the few among the Free Folk still standing. With the war for the Iron Throne no business of his, he leads what remains of his people back north of the Wall when it's over, to the kind of life the South never had room for."],
  },

  "Bronn": {
    1: ["A sellsword with no house and no scruples worth mentioning, hired by Tyrion Lannister as a bodyguard on the road and immediately worth every coin of it. When Catelyn Stark hauls Tyrion before Lysa Arryn's court at the Eyrie and demands trial by combat, Bronn steps up as his champion against one of Lysa's household knights and wins by the simple expedient of not fighting fair, buying Tyrion's freedom and Bronn himself a permanent place in his service."],
    2: ["Commands the wildfire ambush that breaks Stannis's fleet in the bay during the siege of King's Landing, a piece of tactical nastiness that turns the battle before it's properly begun. For services rendered he's knighted afterward, Ser Bronn of the Blackwater, a title he wears with visible amusement at how far a good sellsword can rise."],
    3: ["Still Tyrion's man in a court that trusts neither of them, drinking, sparring with Podrick, and generally getting more use out of King's Landing's underworld than the Lannisters get out of their name. When Tyrion, on trial for a murder he didn't commit, asks him to fight as his champion again, Bronn — offered a match to a wealthy heiress by Cersei instead — chooses the marriage and the money over the sword, with no pretense of guilt about it."],
    4: ["The marriage to Lollys keeps him comfortably out of the one fight Tyrion actually needs him for. He visits the cell before the trial to explain himself man to man, no pretense of guilt in it — a sellsword takes the safer money, that's the whole trade, and Tyrion is furious in a way that doesn't quite manage to be surprise. There is no version of that conversation that ends with Bronn on the sand risking his neck for a friendship, however real, that never once came with a salary attached."],
    5: ["Reassigned to Jaime Lannister for a quiet mission to Dorne to retrieve Myrcella, which turns considerably less quiet once the Sand Snakes of House Martell get involved — Bronn fights one of them to a standstill and comes away with a poisoned scratch that spends several uncomfortable days deciding whether to kill him."],
    6: ["Rides with Jaime to the siege of Riverrun, trading insults with the Blackfish's men from outside the walls while Jaime tries diplomacy first and threats second, comfortable as ever playing the practical half of whatever partnership he's currently in. When word arrives that the Freys have taken the castle by treachery instead, Bronn's just as happy to collect his soldier's pay for a siege nobody in Lannister colors actually had to fight."],
    7: ["Fights for the Lannister column at the Loot Train Battle, nearly burned alive when Daenerys's dragon comes in low over the wagons, and manages — barely — to wrestle one of Qyburn's giant crossbows into position and put a bolt into Drogon before the beast wheels off. It is the closest anyone has come all war to hurting a dragon."],
    8: ["Sent by Cersei with orders to kill Tyrion and Jaime both, a job he never quite gets around to finishing, and after the war is won and the throne changes hands for good, he trades his sword for a seat at the table — walking into the new king's court to claim the lordship and the council post he decided, sensibly, he was always owed."],
  },

  "Davos Seaworth": {
    2: ["Stannis's man in every sense but the title, a smuggler knighted for running onions through a siege, missing the fingertips of one hand for the crime of smuggling before that. He distrusts Melisandre and says so to Stannis's face, which no one else dares, and watches her deliver Renly's death alone in a sea cave. At Blackwater he captains the flagship into the bay, sees the green wildfire swallow the fleet around him, and loses his son Matthos in the water. Stannis abandons the siege before Davos is found; he survives, barely, on driftwood and salt water."],
    3: ["Fished half-drowned from the bay and thrown into Stannis's dungeon anyway: he cannot out-argue a red priestess for a king's ear from outside the room, so he tries to kill her instead, and fails. He learns his letters there, coached through the bars by Princess Shireen, an old smuggler doing his alphabet like a child. When Gendry is brought to Dragonstone to be bled with leeches for a blood magic Davos wants no part of, he rows the boy out to open water and sets him free, and is thrown back in his cell for it — until a raven from the Wall changes what Stannis needs him for."],
    4: ["Made Hand of the King over Melisandre's objections, and spends the season translating Stannis's stubborn honor into the only currency that actually moves it: coin and timing. He talks his king into borrowing from the Iron Bank rather than losing his claim by default, and talks him into marching for the Wall when every other lord would have stayed south chasing the throne instead. At Castle Black he watches Stannis's cavalry break the wildling host in the nick of time, the reward for having read a letter nobody else in the castle took seriously."],
    5: ["Sent ahead to Castle Black to arrange provisions for Stannis's march on Winterfell, and it turns out to be lucky timing of the worst kind — he is behind the Wall's gates, not in the camp, when grief and Melisandre and a stalled march talk Stannis into burning his own daughter for wind and favor. He returns to find Shireen already gone and a war he no longer believes in, and quietly buries what was left of his loyalty to Stannis somewhere in that snow."],
    6: ["Finds Shireen's small burned bones outside the ruin of Stannis's camp and carries that discovery like a blade for the rest of the season. At Castle Black he backs Jon's return from the dead with blunt practicality — dead men don't get to stay retired when the living still need them — and rides the North's back roads drumming up swords for the Battle of the Bastards. Afterward, in front of the victorious lords, he calls Melisandre to account for Shireen and has her banished, on pain of death, from ever setting foot in the North again."],
    7: ["Jon's steadiest counselor now, the one who keeps saying the plain thing everyone else is too proud to say: go to Dragonstone, ask the dragon queen for help, dragonglass is dragonglass wherever you find it. He sails south with Jon, needles Missandei and Tyrion in the same breath he flatters them, and talks his way past a locked gate to get an audience with Daenerys at all. Beyond the Wall on the wight hunt he stays behind with the boats, too old for that particular argument, and is still there when the survivors come stumbling back across the ice."],
    8: ["Master of Ships in every way but name, running supply and strategy for a war he privately expects to lose. He argues for patience against Sansa's hoarding and loses that argument gracefully, mans the defenses at Winterfell through the long night, and lives to see the dawn he doubted would come. In King's Landing afterward he watches the war end in ash rather than victory, and takes a seat on the small council that rebuilds the Seven Kingdoms — Hand's work in every sense but the title, again, this time for a king he actually chose."],
  },

  "Samwell Tarly": {
    1: ["The fat, frightened son his father would rather bury than look at, sent to the Wall with a choice that was never really a choice: the black cloak or a hunting accident. He arrives unable to fight, unable to ride, and becomes the butt of every drill until Jon Snow decides that isn't funny anymore and makes the other recruits leave him be. He confesses to Jon that he's a craven, plainly, without asking for pity, and it wins him the truest friendship the Wall has to offer. He takes his vows under a heart tree in the snow, sworn brother to men who would have laughed him out of Horn Hill."],
    2: ["North with the Great Ranging, made keeper of Castle Black's ravens because he can read and write better than men twice as hard as him. Craster's Keep unsettles him more than the march itself did — the missing boys, the daughters who are also wives — but he says nothing, the way everyone there says nothing. At the Fist of the First Men he watches the Old Bear's army dig in on open ground with the dead massing beyond the firelight, and understands, before most of the officers do, exactly how much danger that is."],
    3: ["The Fist falls in the night and Sam survives by luck and by lying flat in the snow while a White Walker walks past him. Fleeing alone he stumbles into Gilly, hiding with her newborn son at what's left of Craster's Keep after the mutiny, and when a Walker corners them both he stabs it with a dragonglass dagger and watches it shatter into nothing — the first man in a generation to learn, by accident, how to kill one. He gets Gilly and the baby through the haunted forest and in at the Wall's gate, half-dead on his feet."],
    4: ["Hides Gilly and her son at Mole's Town before the wildling assault reaches Castle Black, then stands the wall itself with a crossbow and a hoarded store of dragonglass, killing at least one more of the dead before the night is over. He speaks for Jon Snow in the vote for Lord Commander — plainly, the same way he confessed his own cowardice back in season one — and it tips a deadlocked room. Gilly survives the massacre at Mole's Town hidden in a woodpile, and comes back to him."],
    5: ["Settles into something like a life at Castle Black — Gilly, the baby, small kindnesses that feel enormous behind a wall of ice. When Thenns and mutinous wildlings storm the keep after Jon's parley goes wrong, Sam fights past his own fear to protect Gilly with a dragonglass blade, and it earns him a different kind of respect than his father ever gave him. Maester Aemon dies at his side, and it decides him: he'll go to Oldtown and become a maester himself, useless with a sword but not with what he actually knows. He leaves by ship with Gilly and the baby."],
    6: ["Detours home to Horn Hill on the way to Oldtown and gets a season's worth of his father in a single dinner: Randyll Tarly can barely look at the wildling girl or the bastard child, and makes his contempt for Sam's whole life a matter of table conversation. Sam says nothing to his father's face and everything with his hands afterward, stealing the family's ancestral sword, Heartsbane, before he and Gilly slip out in the night. At the Citadel he starts the tedious, unglamorous work of becoming a maester — copying, shelving, emptying chamber pots — while the world outside gets no less dangerous for his absence."],
    7: ["Buried in Oldtown drudgery, forging his own father's signature to get past a locked archive and reading himself hollow-eyed by candlelight. He drains Jorah Mormont's greyscale by hand against every rule of the Citadel and cures him, the kind of stubborn decency that nearly gets him expelled rather than thanked. Deep in a stolen record, he and Gilly turn up a High Septon's account of a prince's marriage annulled and quietly remarried — the sort of detail no one was ever supposed to write down — and Sam, of everyone who might have found it, is the one who carries it north."],
    8: ["Learns at Winterfell that his father and brother were executed on Daenerys's order for refusing to bend the knee, and has to keep marching toward the same war she's fighting anyway. He stands the line at the Battle of Winterfell with a sword he still isn't very good with, goes down under a pile of the dead, and lives only because Edd puts a spear through the thing on top of him and dies doing it. Afterward he sits on the small council that rebuilds Westeros, proposes letting the commons choose their own rulers to a room that laughs at him, and writes the history everyone else in this story will be remembered by — a stack of pages he calls A Song of Ice and Fire."],
  },

  "Petyr Baelish": {
    1: ["Master of Coin, owner of half the city's brothels, and a man who has spent twenty years turning an old humiliation at Riverrun into the most useful information network in Westeros. He is helpful to Ned Stark in exactly the ways that serve him and no others, offering the gold cloaks' loyalty as a way into Ned's confidence, then selling that same confidence to Cersei the instant it's worth more to her than to him. He watches Ned die from the crowd, which is where he prefers to watch everything."],
    2: ["Still Master of Coin and still trading in secrets nobody else can obtain, threading himself between Cersei, Tyrion and the Tyrells without committing to any of them further than the next piece of leverage requires. He needles Tyrion about the danger of assumptions in a room full of them, courts Margaery's family on the crown's behalf, and lets his interest in Sansa Stark show just enough to be noticed and not enough to be stopped. He survives Blackwater by simply not being there for it, having made himself useful to whichever side won before the battle was even fought."],
    3: ["Brokers a Tyrell-Stark match that would keep Sansa out of Joffrey's reach and out of his own, then quietly ensures it never happens, because he has other plans for her entirely. He leaves King's Landing for the Eyrie to marry Lysa Arryn, a woman he has kept on a string for years with letters and unmet promises, and takes the Vale's power for himself the moment the vows are said. On his way out of the capital he stops to have a quiet word with Olenna Tyrell — the kind of conversation whose purpose only becomes clear much later."],
    4: ["Reappears in King's Landing for Joffrey's wedding and leaves it a killer, if not by his own hand then by his own design. In the chaos after the boy king dies he spirits Sansa out of the city by ship, cloak and all, and carries her to the Eyrie as his ward. When Lysa, jealous and unraveling, threatens Sansa at the Moon Door, Littlefinger talks his wife right up to the edge of it and pushes her through, then tells the Vale's assembled lords a story about grief and suicide that not one of them dares question."],
    5: ["Lord Protector of the Vale in all but name, and increasingly of the North too — he marries Sansa to Ramsay Bolton to buy her a claim on Winterfell and himself a foothold in the war to come, and leaves her there with a promise he can't actually keep, that he'll come back for her if it goes wrong. It goes wrong. He spends the rest of the season away in the Vale managing lords who'd rather not fight anyone, while the marriage he arranged turns into something considerably worse than politics."],
    6: ["Brings the knights of the Vale north on his own initiative, a private army he's been building for exactly this kind of leverage, and arrives at the Battle of the Bastards late enough to look prudent and early enough to matter — his cavalry breaks Ramsay's line after Jon's forces have already paid for it in bodies. He tells Sansa, alone, that he pictures the two of them ruling from the Iron Throne together, and she lets him say it without agreeing to a word of it. By season's end she is Lady of Winterfell, and he is still working out whether that was his plan or hers."],
    7: ["Spends the season doing what he's always done — planting doubt, this time between two sisters who grew up distrusting each other for reasons that have nothing to do with him and everything to do with how useful that distrust is to him. He needles Arya about an old letter Sansa once wrote, and needles Sansa about Arya's list, certain that suspicion between them keeps him necessary to both. Sansa and Arya compare notes instead of knives, lay the pieces of his game out in front of Winterfell's whole household, and produce, as the final exhibit, a Valyrian steel dagger he lost in a bet years ago — the one that was used to try to kill Bran. Arya cuts his throat before he finishes asking for a chance to explain."],
  },

  "Varys": {
    1: ["The eunuch spymaster, soft-spoken and never quite trustworthy, whose 'little birds' hear everything said in King's Landing before the speaker has even finished the sentence. He offers Ned Stark warnings dressed up as riddles about who really holds power in the city, and when Ned is arrested he visits the black cells to argue — not entirely dishonestly — that a false confession is the only move left that might save his daughters. Ned takes the advice, confesses, and loses his head anyway, a demonstration of how little even the best information is worth once a boy with a crown stops listening to reason."],
    2: ["Tyrion's most useful and least trustworthy ally as acting Hand, forever fencing with Littlefinger in corridors over which of them actually understands the city better. He tells Tyrion, unprompted, the story of the sorcerer who cut him as a boy and burned the pieces in a brazier, a confidence that explains a great deal about a man who trusts no one and needs nothing from anyone. During the Battle of Blackwater he stays inside the walls doing the unglamorous work that wins sieges — organizing, provisioning, making sure the city that survives the night still has someone competent running it."],
    3: ["Keeps his allegiance quietly tilted toward Tyrion and away from Joffrey and Cersei, while somewhere across the water a khaleesi with three dragons starts to look like a bet worth placing early. The man who cut him as a boy sends an assassin after him in a box marked as a gift; Varys turns the tables, traps his old tormentor inside a chest instead, and has him shipped off to a slow death at sea — the closest thing to a smile he allows himself all season."],
    4: ["After Tyrion is condemned for a murder he didn't commit, Varys helps smuggle him out of the city in a crate bound for the docks, gambling his own position and possibly his life on a man he has never fully vouched for before. He tells Tyrion where to find Illyrio's ship, and where to find Illyrio's other project, before boarding it himself. By the time King's Landing wakes up to Tywin Lannister's murder, both of them are already at sea, headed for a continent Varys has been quietly betting on since before anyone knew a queen was coming from it."],
    5: ["Crosses the Narrow Sea with Tyrion, keeping him drunk, alive, and moving through a string of near-disasters that would have killed a less-connected man twice over. He talks his way past pirates and slavers with the same unbothered patience he used to bring to Small Council meetings, and steers Tyrion, deliberately, toward Meereen and the queen he's been quietly championing from a distance for years. By the time they reach her city, the eunuch who once served three kings he never respected finally has a ruler he's willing to work for outright."],
    6: ["Established now as Daenerys's Master of Whisperers in practice if not yet in name, and put to the use he's always been best at: recruiting allies she can't reach herself. Sent to Dorne, he helps turn two of the crown's oldest grudges — the Martells and the Tyrells — into an alliance against the Lannisters, all without Daenerys lifting a finger. He returns to Meereen in time to sail west with her fleet, dragons and all, a spymaster who spent years collecting favors finally about to collect on every one of them at once."],
    7: ["Formally Hand-adjacent counsel at Dragonstone now, pushing Daenerys toward patience and alliance-building over spectacle, which puts him at odds with a queen who has spent her whole life being told to wait. When she demands to know where his loyalty truly lies, he gives her the only honest answer he's ever given anyone — not to a house or a crown, but to the people who have to live with whichever one wins — and it's convincing enough, or useful enough, to keep his head on his shoulders for one more season."],
    8: ["Watches Daenerys lose Missandei, Jorah, and finally her own restraint in the same handful of weeks, and does the arithmetic a spymaster can't help doing: a monarch this destabilized is a danger to the realm he claims to actually serve. He tries, carefully and too late, to steer support toward Jon instead, and is caught doing it — reported by the one person in her camp he never suspected would report him. Daenerys has him burned on the beach at Dragonstone, and he goes to it without much fight, a man who spent his whole life surviving kings finally undone by choosing, once, to work against one."],
  },

  "Melisandre": {
    2: ["The red priestess at Stannis Baratheon's side, convinced he is a prophesied hero reborn and willing to burn anyone who says otherwise, up to and including old gods' statues and Stannis's own brother. She gives Stannis a son made of shadow and smoke to murder Renly in his own tent, and stands alone in a sea cave to do it. At Blackwater she promises victory in the flames and is wrong, or at least wrong about the timing, and Stannis's fleet burns on the bay while she watches from a safe distance."],
    3: ["Takes Gendry from Dragonstone's dungeons because his king's blood is useful for a working older and darker than anything Stannis has tried before — leeches, a fire, and three names read into it. She loses Gendry to Davos's conscience before the ritual can run its second, worse course, and by the season's end her attention has started drifting north, past Stannis's throne entirely, toward a wall and a war that a red priestess reads more clearly in the flames than any lord currently sitting in Westeros."],
    4: ["Marches north with Stannis's host, working on Selyse and unsettling Castle Black's Watch, who have never had a priestess of R'hllor lecturing them about their god's plans for the realm. She tells Jon Snow, without much subtlety, that he interests her far more than a king who has already disappointed her once, and it isn't entirely about prophecy. When Stannis's cavalry arrives to break the wildling siege at the last possible moment, she takes it as proof she picked correctly again, and doesn't dwell on how close it came to going the other way."],
    5: ["Presses Stannis toward the darkest math of the whole war: a king's blood sacrifice, his own daughter's, to buy wind and favor for a march on Winterfell that has stalled in the snow. Selyse breaks first and then doesn't stop him; Melisandre lights the pyre herself and watches Shireen burn while the camp listens to a child scream. It works, in the narrowest sense — the snow stops — and it costs Stannis every soldier who hears about it by morning, and costs Melisandre, eventually, a debt she hasn't started paying yet."],
    6: ["Retreats to Castle Black a believer whose worst working has just failed her completely, and finds a dead Lord Commander laid out on a table with nothing left to lose. She resurrects Jon Snow with a prayer she doesn't fully expect to work, and it works anyway, which unsettles her as much as it does anyone else in the room. Davos, who buried a burned girl because of her, corners her afterward with the truth about Shireen, and when Jon's council learns what she did, she is banished from the North on pain of death and rides south not knowing, for the first time in years, what R'hllor actually wants from her."],
    7: ["Arrives at Dragonstone just long enough to tell Daenerys what a red priestess is for, urges her toward Jon Snow as the ally her war actually needs, and leaves again for Volantis before the alliance she suggested has even been tested. On her way out she tells Varys, almost gently, that she still has one more part left to play in Westeros, and that she'll die there — which he takes, correctly, as neither a boast nor a threat but simply an itinerary."],
    8: ["Returns to Winterfell uninvited on the eve of the Long Night, lights the Dothraki's arakhs and the trench around the castle with a word, and spends the battle doing the only thing left that a priestess of a fire god can usefully do against an army of ice. She tells Arya, in passing, the kind of thing she once told Jon Snow — that she sees deaths in strange colors, brown eyes and blue and green — and it matters more than either of them realizes that night. When the dead are finally beaten back, she walks out past the gates into the snow, removes the necklace that has kept her young for a hundred years, and dies exactly where she said she would."],
  },

  "Gilly": {
    2: ["One of Craster's daughter-wives at his keep beyond the Wall, pregnant and resigned to a life that has taught her not to expect kindness, from her father or from anyone else. She begs the Night's Watch rangers passing through for help if her baby is born a boy, because she knows exactly what Craster does with sons and none of the sworn brothers eating at his table want to admit they know it too. Sam is the only one who looks at her like a person rather than a problem to get past, and it's not nothing."],
    3: ["Gives birth alone at Craster's Keep while the Watch mutinies against the Old Bear in the yard outside, and loses the argument over her own son's fate to Craster until the chaos of the mutiny makes the argument moot. Sam gets her and the baby out in the confusion, kills a White Walker with a dragonglass blade to do it, and drags all three of them south through a haunted forest with wildlings and worse behind them. She reaches the Wall on her own feet, half-frozen, still holding the baby she was told she'd never get to keep."],
    4: ["Hidden at Mole's Town for her own safety while Sam mans the Wall against the wildling assault, and it is nearly the wrong call — Ygritte's raiders tear through the village and Gilly survives only by hiding herself and the baby in an empty oven while everyone around her is killed. She makes her way to Castle Black afterward in shock, and Sam, who spent the battle certain he'd sent her to die, finds her alive at the gate. It is the closest either of them comes to losing everything without a sword being involved."],
    5: ["Settles into a version of safety at Castle Black that neither of them quite trusts yet, close enough to Sam that it becomes something more than gratitude. When Alliser Thorne's mutineers and a band of Thenns storm the keep, she fights alongside Sam rather than simply hiding behind him, and it's the first time either of them treats her as anything other than someone who needs protecting. When Sam decides to go to Oldtown and become a maester, she and the baby go with him, onto a ship bound for a city that has never had any use for wildlings."],
    6: ["Brought to Horn Hill and introduced to Sam's family as exactly what she is, and Randyll Tarly makes his contempt for that plain over dinner, in front of his son and grandchild both. Gilly holds her ground at the table in a way Sam himself can't quite manage, and it's her presence as much as his father's cruelty that finally decides him — he steals the family sword and they leave together in the night. In Oldtown she and the baby wait through Sam's long apprenticeship in a small rented room, a wildling girl learning to read in a city built on believing people like her don't."],
    7: ["Still teaching herself to read in Oldtown, and it's Gilly, not Sam, who stumbles on the detail that matters most — an old High Septon's account, read aloud half as a curiosity, of a prince's marriage annulled and remarried in secret. Sam barely registers it at the time, too tired of copying scrolls to notice he's holding the answer to a question half of Westeros is fighting over. By the end of the season she's had enough of the Citadel's slow uselessness in a fast war, and leaves for Winterfell with Sam and the baby, pregnant again herself."],
    8: ["Arrives at Winterfell a stranger among lords, pregnant, with a small son who has never seen snow like this before, and settles in as if daring anyone to comment on any of it. During the Battle of Winterfell she shelters in the crypts with the rest of the women, children, and old men, which turns out to be far from the safest place in the castle once the dead start climbing out of the very stone around them. She survives it, and by the season's end she is still at Sam's side, waiting on a second child and whatever quieter life a maester's family gets to have now."],
  },

  "Missandei": {
    3: ["A slave translator working for Kraznys mo Nakloz in Astapor, softening his contempt into something more diplomatic each time she renders it into the Common Tongue for Daenerys — sparing her, or trying to. It turns out not to matter: Daenerys has understood the Astapori Valyrian all along and has heard every insult unfiltered, and uses that same language to turn the Unsullied against their masters in front of the whole city, Missandei included. Freed along with the army when Astapor falls, she has nowhere obvious left to go and stays anyway, choosing Daenerys over a home she hasn't seen since she was a child."],
    4: ["In Meereen now, teaching Grey Worm and the other freed Unsullied to read and speak the Common Tongue in whatever hours the war doesn't take, and finding in him someone as careful with words as she is. She advises Daenerys on the business of ruling former slaves who don't agree among themselves on what freedom should look like, translating not just language now but temperament — whose anger is real, whose grief is real, whose flattery isn't. It's slow, unglamorous work, and she does it without complaint, steadier than almost anyone else around the queen."],
    5: ["Meereen curdles around her — Sons of the Harpy in the alleys, masters plotting in daylight, a fighting pit Daenerys reopens against her own better judgment. When Grey Worm is carried back near death from an ambush, she sits with him and admits, plainly, that she would have grieved him, which is as close as either of them has come to saying more. She holds the city together with Tyrion and the rest of the small council once Daenerys vanishes on dragonback, running a government that, for a while, has no queen in it."],
    6: ["Holds Meereen with Grey Worm and Tyrion while the masters' fleet burns the harbor, cool under pressure in a room full of men losing theirs, and helps talk Daenerys back from torching the city's remaining enemies without a plan first. When the fleet finally sails for Westeros she goes with it, translator no longer strictly needed but advisor very much still."],
    7: ["At Dragonstone as the councils get larger and the arguments get sharper, still the one person Daenerys trusts to say a hard thing plainly rather than prettily. Before the Unsullied sail for Casterly Rock, she and Grey Worm finally say aloud what the last few years have only implied — unhurried about it, because there has never been a good time and they've stopped waiting for one."],
    8: ["North to Winterfell for the fight against the dead, then south again with the fleet toward King's Landing — and the fleet is the trap. Euron's ships take her before she ever reaches shore, and Cersei has her walked out onto the Red Keep's walls in chains and beheaded in front of Daenerys and Grey Worm both, her last word — Dracarys — less a plea than an instruction."],
  },

  "Grey Worm": {
    3: ["One of the Unsullied Daenerys buys wholesale from Kraznys mo Nakloz in Astapor, and among the first to step forward once she turns the army against its masters and offers every soldier in it a real choice for the first time in his life — walk away free, or stay and serve her freely. He stays, and keeps the name Grey Worm, a slave-name meant to shame him, because it was the name he was wearing the day he became a free man, which he decides makes it lucky rather than something to be rid of. Command of the Unsullied falls to him afterward, and it suits him."],
    4: ["Settles into command of Daenerys's army properly now, methodical about sieges and garrisons, and spends what free hours he has learning letters and the Common Tongue from Missandei — careful, awkward lessons that seem to matter to him more than he lets on. Meereen is his to help hold once it falls, and it is a city that does not want holding."],
    5: ["Ambushed in a Meereenese alley by the Sons of the Harpy alongside Ser Barristan Selmy — Barristan doesn't survive it, and Grey Worm is carried back near death, saved only because Missandei reaches him in time. Recovering, he tells her, more plainly than he has said anything to anyone in years, that what troubled him most about nearly dying wasn't missing the fight — it was coming close to dying without ever telling her how he felt. It's the most words he's spent on the subject in two years, and he means all of them."],
    6: ["Back on his feet and back in the field, he and Tyrion out-think the masters' siege of Meereen while Daenerys is away, luring their fleet into the harbor and burning it rather than meeting it ship for ship. Boards the fleet bound for Westeros afterward, Missandei beside him, pointed at last toward a war he actually chose."],
    7: ["Leads the Unsullied against Casterly Rock exactly as planned and takes it in a morning — only to learn the Lannister army was never inside it, and the Rock was never really the point. Marches back east through a countryside that's turned hostile, and before it's done, finally lets himself and Missandei have the conversation they've been circling for years."],
    8: ["Survives the Battle of Winterfell and marches south with what's left of the army to finish the war properly — except the fleet is ambushed before it lands, and Missandei is taken and executed on the walls of King's Landing with him watching helplessly from the beach. What happens to the city once its bells ring surrender a few days later is not strategy anymore; Grey Worm leads it anyway, and does not look like a man interested in being talked out of it. Afterward he holds out for justice against the ones responsible, and in the end sails for Naath with the Unsullied who are done fighting other people's wars — Missandei's home standing in, at least, for his own."],
  },

  "Olenna Tyrell": {
    3: ["Arrives in King's Landing for the business of marrying her granddaughter well, and wastes no time letting Tywin Lannister know she is neither intimidated by him nor impressed by the crown's finances, needling him about Lannister debts to Highgarden with open relish. Spots an opportunity to marry Loras to Sansa Stark and secure a claim on Winterfell through her, and loses the idea within days when Tywin outmaneuvers her by marrying Sansa to Tyrion instead — a rare defeat, and one she doesn't pretend to enjoy."],
    4: ["Margaery marries Joffrey at last, and the wedding does not survive the feast — Joffrey chokes and dies at the high table, and among the small, easily missed details of that afternoon is Olenna leaning in to admire the hairnet Sansa Stark is wearing, and a stone that isn't there anymore by the time anyone thinks to look for it. Whatever really happened, Margaery is free of one husband and being fitted for a kinder one within days, and Olenna does not so much as blink through any of it."],
    5: ["Watches the High Sparrow's Faith Militant return to King's Landing with a foreboding nobody else at court seems to share, and is proven right within the season — Loras arrested on old charges revived new, Margaery arrested alongside him for lying under oath to protect her brother, and Tommen too besotted and too outmaneuvered to do more than plead. Olenna argues the High Sparrow to a standstill and gets nothing for it, and leaves the capital in disgust rather than watch a king she considers useless fail to save her family."],
    6: ["Returns to King's Landing for Margaery's walk of penance, expecting to see her granddaughter through the ordeal — and instead watches the Sept of Baelor go up in wildfire with Margaery, Loras, and half the city's high lords inside, Cersei's doing, though Cersei is never made to answer for it that day. What remains of House Tyrell she puts behind Daenerys Targaryen's war, not out of any love for dragons but because Cersei has made an enemy of the one Tyrell who was never going to let a debt like this one go unpaid."],
    7: ["Holds Highgarden in the name of Daenerys's alliance while the queen's attention is elsewhere, and it costs her the city — Jaime Lannister marches on it while the main Tyrell strength is off supplying Dany's army, and takes it in a rout. Cornered, Olenna refuses to run and refuses to beg, and when Jaime offers her a clean death by poison instead of a public one, she takes the cup without flinching and spends her last minutes telling him, precisely and with some satisfaction, exactly whose hand put the poison in Joffrey's cup all those years ago — and making sure Cersei is never to know it was her."],
  },

  "Margaery Tyrell": {
    2: ["Arrives at Renly Baratheon's war camp already married to him, poised and pragmatic about a marriage that is politics dressed as romance, and unbothered by what the marriage actually is behind closed doors. When Renly is murdered by the shadow Melisandre births, Margaery loses a husband and, with her family, promptly sets about arranging a better one — the Iron Throne looks a great deal more reachable through Joffrey Baratheon than it ever did through Renly, and she and her grandmother move toward it within days of the funeral."],
    3: ["Engaged to Joffrey now, and visibly better at being watched by a crowd than either of the Lannisters she's marrying into — visits orphanages and war hospitals, praises the smallfolk to their faces, and lets Joffrey believe every bit of goodwill it earns her is really about him. Wins over young Tommen with nothing more than a kitten and some patient attention, laying groundwork nobody but her grandmother seems to notice she's laying. Cersei watches her with open suspicion and gets nothing back but courtesy, which needles Cersei considerably more than an argument would."],
    4: ["Marries Joffrey at last in a wedding built to be remembered, and it is — he collapses choking at his own feast and dies in front of the whole court, leaving Margaery a widow before the wine's finished pouring. Grief lasts about as long as the funeral; within days the Tyrells and Cersei alike are discussing a match with Tommen instead, and Margaery is visiting the new king in the training yard before the old one is cold in his tomb."],
    5: ["Marries Tommen and sets about being the wife to him she could never quite be to his brother — warmth this time, or close enough to it that Tommen can't tell the difference, which is rather the point. Wars quietly with Cersei for the boy king's attention and wins, easily, simply by being kinder to him than his mother knows how to be. It costs her: the High Sparrow's Faith Militant, revived with Cersei's blessing, arrests Loras on old charges and then arrests Margaery too, for swearing under oath that she knew nothing about them."],
    6: ["Held by the Faith through the winter, alternately defiant and, eventually, convincingly pious — enough that by the time she's released she has both the High Sparrow's trust and Tommen's ear, and appears, to everyone including Tommen, to have found real religion. What she's actually doing is running out the clock, trying to walk her family and her husband's throne through the Sparrow's grip without a war, and she comes closer to managing it than anyone expects — right up until the Sept of Baelor and everyone inside it goes up in wildfire beneath her."],
  },

  "Ramsay Bolton": {
    3: ["Bastard of Roose Bolton, and the reason Theon Greyjoy never makes it home after Winterfell burns — his men catch Theon fleeing north, and Ramsay keeps him at the Dreadfort as an ongoing project, dismantling him piece by piece with a knife and a great deal of patience, offering just enough mercy between cruelties to make Theon grateful for its absence. Nobody at Winterfell or beyond knows what's become of Theon, or that the pleasant, soft-spoken man doing it to him is baseborn, unacknowledged, and determined not to stay either much longer."],
    4: ["Sent to finish taking Moat Cailin from its ironborn garrison, and does it without a real battle — parades what's left of Theon, thoroughly broken and answering only to Reek, to talk the garrison into surrendering, then puts them to the sword the moment they lay down their weapons anyway. It earns him what he actually wants: a royal decree legitimizing him as Ramsay Bolton, trueborn in name if nothing else, with his father's house and eventually his father's seat somewhere in view."],
    5: ["Marries Sansa Stark on his father's orders, to bind Stark lands to the Bolton name, and the wedding night is as brutal as anything he's done to Theon, with Theon forced to watch. The abuse continues for weeks after, and when Sansa manages to get word north for help and then tries to flee with Theon, the season leaves them jumping from Winterfell's walls into the dark with dogs on their trail. In between, when Stannis Baratheon marches on Winterfell, Ramsay slips out with a small raiding party and burns his siege camp's supplies and horses, gutting Stannis's army before it ever reaches the castle."],
    6: ["Hunts Sansa and Theon's trail with hounds, and when his father finally has a trueborn son by his new wife, Ramsay resolves the problem the direct way, with a knife for Roose and the hounds again for the wife and infant, and stands unchallenged as Lord of the Dreadfort and Winterfell both. Rides out to meet Jon Snow's scratch army at the Battle of the Bastards and opens the engagement by putting an arrow through Rickon Stark, calm and unhurried, mid-field. Loses anyway to the Vale's cavalry and Jon's fury, is beaten bloody in his own dooryard, and Sansa gets the last word — she leaves him for his own hounds, kept hungry for the occasion, to finish in their kennel."],
  },

  "Roose Bolton": {
    1: ["A Northern bannerman in Robb Stark's war council, quiet, precise, and prone to mentioning his bastard son up at the Dreadfort so casually that nobody thinks to ask more about him. Recommends leeches over speed and is usually right; present at the Whispering Wood when Robb's ambush captures Jaime Lannister, offering counsel that is always sound and never warm."],
    2: ["Given Harrenhal to hold once Tywin abandons it, and runs the ruined castle as a grim, undermanned garrison, more concerned with the war's arithmetic than with the prisoners passing through it — among them a girl working under a false name, though he never has reason to look twice at her."],
    3: ["Brought into the Freys' plan for Robb Stark's wedding feast by Tywin's offer of the North as payment, and stands inside the hall at the Twins while Robb and Catelyn Stark are killed under his own roof's hospitality — the last thing Robb sees is the fact that a man he trusted was in on it. Named Warden of the North for his trouble."],
    4: ["Rules the North from the Dreadfort as its new Warden, more concerned with consolidating a conquest nobody else in the North accepts than with comfort, and sends his son Ramsay to finish taking Moat Cailin from the ironborn — backing whatever ugliness gets the job done, so long as it works."],
    5: ["Has taken a new wife, Walda Frey, for the trueborn heir Ramsay isn't, and treats his bastard's usefulness and his bastard's danger as the same fact, to be managed rather than resolved. Approves Ramsay's marriage to Sansa Stark as a way to make Bolton rule of the North look legitimate rather than merely won, and holds Winterfell against Stannis Baratheon's advance by letting Ramsay handle the parts of the war he has no stomach to watch himself."],
    6: ["Walda gives him the trueborn son he's waited years for, and he has perhaps a day to enjoy it before Ramsay, unwilling to share Winterfell with a brother, puts a knife in his father's stomach and tells him, almost gently, that his time is up."],
  },

  "Joffrey Baratheon": {
    1: ["Robert's heir in name, blond in a family of black-haired Baratheons, and the first thread anyone pulls on the way to what Cersei and Jaime have done. He is a boy raised to believe that wanting a thing is the same as being owed it: he goads Arya into a fight by the Trident, lets Mycah die for it, and stands over Ned in the throne room to take back a promise of mercy in front of the crowd he wants clapping. Robert's death makes him king before he has learned to fear anyone, and the first thing he does with the crown is take Ned Stark's head, live, with Sansa watching, because he likes how it looks."],
    2: ["King now, and delighted by it in ways that curdle fast: he has Sansa beaten for her brother's victories and stripped in open court until Tyrion intervenes, and marks his name day with a joke that leaves men dead in the yard. He wants the wildfire that burns Stannis's fleet to be his own idea and lets Tyrion do the actual thinking behind it. When the city turns on him during the bread riots he takes a handful of filth to the face and remembers exactly who was standing next to him — not fondly. At Blackwater he watches the battle from the walls with his uncle for as long as it looks like winning, then goes to hide in his mother's rooms the moment it stops."],
    3: ["Betrothed now to Margaery Tyrell, who manages him so smoothly he mistakes her indulgence for admiration, and furious that Sansa has been married off to Tyrion rather than left for him to torment on his own schedule. When word reaches King's Landing of the Red Wedding, he brings Sansa a book of the realm's great kings so he can walk her through an illustration of her brother's death, page by page, because taking things from people is the only intimacy he understands. Tyrion's wedding gift to him is a book too, on the reigns of past kings; Joffrey does not appreciate being outmaneuvered at his own game, and says so, loudly."],
    4: ["His wedding to Margaery is the biggest show King's Landing has staged in years, and he spends it needling Tyrion in front of the whole court — makes him cupbearer, pours wine over his head, restages the War of the Five Kings with dwarfs for entertainment — because it is the last day he will ever get to be the smallest cruelty in the room. He chokes on his own wedding wine partway through the pie course, claws at his throat in front of five hundred witnesses, and dies in his mother's arms with his eyes full of blood, one finger pointed at his uncle."],
  },

  "Tommen Baratheon": {
    4: ["Joffrey's little brother, gentler by miles and aware of it, inherits a crown he never asked for and a grandfather who moves fast to make sure grief does not slow down an alliance — he is married to Margaery within the same season his brother dies at her wedding feast. Tywin walks him through what a king owes his lords like a man teaching a child to tie his boots, and Margaery, newly widowed of a husband who barely touched her, starts teaching him a different set of lessons in his bedroom at night, patient and entirely unhurried."],
    5: ["Married and besotted, and easy for both his wife and his mother to manage as long as neither one notices the other doing it. Cersei's gamble to rearm the Faith and aim it at the Tyrells backfires immediately: Margaery is arrested on the Sept steps for lying to protect her brother, and Tommen marches the Kingsguard down to get her out, only to be turned away by the High Sparrow with nothing but words — because it turns out a king who has never had to fight for anything does not know how. He spends the back half of the year locked out of both his wife's cell and his own city's temple."],
    6: ["King in name only by now, and it shows: he lets Cersei bar him from attending his own wife's trial in the Sept, and it is Margaery who has to send him a warning through the Faith to stay away that morning without saying why. He watches the Sept of Baelor go up from the Red Keep window, a green column of fire that takes Margaery and half the court he has spent two years failing to protect along with it, and once it settles he sets his crown on the table and walks out through the same window, without a word to anyone."],
  },

  "Myrcella Baratheon": {
    1: ["The younger, quieter version of her family — closer to Sansa than to either of her brothers, and the one Baratheon child who seems to notice when the people around her are unhappy. She watches her father's court from its edges for most of the season, and by the end of it Tyrion has traded her away to Dorne as a bride for Prince Trystane Martell, a piece moved to secure an alliance rather than a happiness. She leaves King's Landing without much say in the matter, young enough that no one thinks to ask her opinion of it."],
    5: ["Grown now, genuinely fond of Trystane rather than merely assigned to him, and settled enough in Dorne that when Jaime arrives in secret to bring her home she does not particularly want to go. Ellaria's grief over Oberyn very nearly gets her killed by the Sand Snakes before Prince Doran steps in to stop it, and on the boat back to King's Landing Jaime finally tells her the truth about her parentage — that he is her father, not her uncle — and she tells him she already knew, and that it changes nothing about how she feels about him. Ellaria's goodbye kiss on the dock turns out to have been the real weapon all along: Myrcella bleeds from the nose crossing the water and dies in Jaime's arms within sight of home."],
  },

  "Renly Baratheon": {
    1: ["The youngest of Robert's brothers, better looking than either of his siblings and better liked at court for it, with Loras Tyrell for a companion that everyone pretends not to notice is more than that. He tells Ned, while Robert lies dying upstairs, that the throne is there for whichever of them moves first and has the gold cloaks behind him — arrest Cersei, take the boy hostage, settle it before anyone else even knows Robert is gone. Ned refuses on principle, and Renly, unwilling to wait around for that principle to get him killed too, rides for Storm's End that same night, leaving the capital to Cersei and Stannis's unspoken quarrel over what's left of it."],
    2: ["King of his own invention, with a hundred thousand Tyrell swords behind the claim and Loras still very much beside him in more than just the war council. Catelyn Stark comes to his camp asking for an alliance and leaves unimpressed; Brienne of Tarth beats Loras in single combat at a tournament and asks for nothing but a place in his guard, and gets it. He rides to parley with Stannis at Storm's End certain his numbers make him untouchable, and that same night, in his own tent, a shadow with Stannis's face and none of his solidity opens his throat while Brienne watches and cannot stop it."],
  },

  "Selyse Baratheon": {
    2: ["Stannis's wife in name and the Lord of Light's the rest of the way, burning the statues of the Seven on the beach at Dragonstone with a fervor her husband can only manage secondhand. She keeps her distance from Shireen, whose face carries the scars of the greyscale that nearly killed her as a baby, as though the girl's survival were an embarrassment rather than a mercy. Whatever marriage she and Stannis have left runs entirely through Melisandre now, and Selyse seems to have made her peace with that, or at least convinced herself she has."],
    3: ["Little changes for her at Dragonstone except the arrival of Gendry, whose blood Melisandre wants for a ritual meant to kill three pretender kings, and Selyse watches the leeching with the same fixed devotion she brings to everything the red priestess asks of her. Down the corridor from her own chambers sit jars holding the children she lost before Shireen lived, kept and labeled rather than buried — a private grief she has folded so completely into her faith that it no longer reads as grief at all."],
    4: ["She crosses to the Wall with the rest of Stannis's household when his war shifts north, arriving in time to see his cavalry break the wildling siege of Castle Black from the tree line like something out of Melisandre's fire. She is as unmoved by Jon Snow's account of what is coming from beyond the Wall as she is by most things that are not the Lord of Light, and stands at Stannis's shoulder while he weighs a march on Winterfell that will need every sword and horse he has left."],
    5: ["The march on Winterfell stalls in the snow, the horses die faster than they can be replaced, and Ramsay's night raid burns what little food is left, and it is in that arithmetic that Melisandre finally gets what she has wanted since Renly's tent — a king's blood, offered willingly, to turn the war. Selyse agrees to the plan in the abstract, the way she agrees to anything Melisandre proposes, and only understands what it means once Shireen is actually being walked to the pyre, screaming for her; she tries to stop it then and is held back by her own husband's men. When the army breaks and the camp empties out, she is found hanging in her own tent, the last of the household with nothing left to believe in."],
  },

  "Shireen Baratheon": {
    2: ["Stannis's only child, marked on one cheek by the greyscale that nearly killed her as a baby and treated by half of Dragonstone as though the scars were catching. She keeps mostly to the edges of a household more interested in her father's war than in her own company, quietly reading in a castle not built for children, until Stannis comes home from Blackwater beaten and finds her waiting up for him anyway — and gives her, for once, something like tenderness instead of duty."],
    3: ["Davos ends up in the dungeon for freeing Gendry against Melisandre's wishes, and Shireen is the one who visits him there, again and again, bringing books and teaching a grown man his letters with the patience of someone used to being the only person in the castle willing to be kind to somebody. She tells him the story of the Dance of the Dragons as though it were the best thing she owns, because to her it is, and Davos listens like it matters, because by then it does."],
    4: ["She crosses to the Wall with the rest of the household and finds, in Gilly, someone else young and out of place in a fortress full of soldiers, and gives her lessons the same way she once gave them to Davos. When Davos needs to talk the Iron Bank out of calling in a dead king's debts, it is Shireen who finds him the historical precedent to build the letter around, quietly doing the diplomacy her father is too proud to manage himself."],
    5: ["She comes north with the army this time, for reasons no one quite explains to her, rather than staying behind at Dragonstone under Selyse's grip as she always has before. On the road she and Stannis play cyvasse most evenings, and in one of the only unguarded conversations he has all series, he tells her plainly that she is his daughter and he would not change a single thing about her, scars included. Weeks later, with the horses dead and the snow refusing to break and Melisandre insisting a king's blood is the only thing left that can turn the war, he lets them walk her to the stake anyway, and does not look away."],
  },

  "Balon Greyjoy": {
    1: ["Lord Reaper of Pyke, and no gentler with his last living son than the years apart made him. Theon returns from the mainland as Robb Stark's envoy, offering the old alliance in exchange for ships against the Lannisters, and Balon receives him first with silence and then with contempt — a boy raised soft in a wolf's hall, come home to ask for a war fought on someone else's terms. He burns Robb's letter in the brazier, tells Theon that Ned Stark's roof cost him more than he knows, and turns instead to Yara, whose account of Pyke's readiness he trusts over her brother's. He means to have his own war, not Robb's."],
    2: ["Sends Yara to take Deepwood Motte and lets Theon go raiding the coastline with lesser men and a smaller fleet, the difference in the assignments itself a verdict on which child he rates higher. When word reaches Pyke that Theon has seized Winterfell instead — undefended, held by boys, worth nothing to the war — Balon does not take it as conquest; he reads it as vanity dressed up as strategy, a raid that will draw the whole North down on a garrison of twenty men, and says so plainly. He sends no further ships to help hold what his son has taken."],
    6: ["Years off the page while the war in the south runs its course, and he returns to it standing on a rope bridge over the sea, mid-storm, arguing with the brother he exiled long ago. Euron has come home from years of piracy to Pyke, mocking the driftwood crown and the war it bought, and Balon meets the old resentment with the flat authority of a man still used to being obeyed. Euron does not argue back. He puts a hand to his brother's chest and drops him into the sea, and the storm covers the sound of it; Pyke wakes to a Kingsmoot instead of a king."],
  },

  "Yara Greyjoy": {
    2: ["Sent by her father to take Deepwood Motte, and does it without ceremony, holding the castle while her younger brother goes chasing glory of his own choosing. She rides into Winterfell after Theon has already claimed it, sees exactly what it is — an empty seat, twenty men, and a Stark boy's castle held by a Greyjoy nobody sent there for that purpose — and offers to take her brother home rather than let him drown in it. He refuses her. She leaves him to it, and the choice will cost them both more than either expects."],
    3: ["A box arrives at Pyke addressed to her father, containing proof of what has been done to her brother at the Dreadfort, and the message underneath it is plain: this is what becomes of Greyjoys who reach for more than the sea gives them. Balon receives it as confirmation of what he already suspected and writes Theon off as spent. Yara does not. She sits through her father's verdict without arguing him out of it in the hall, already working out, in private, what she is willing to spend to prove him wrong."],
    6: ["Pyke buries a king it never mourned and calls a Kingsmoot to choose the next one, and Yara makes her case with the plainest platform in the hall — enough wars fought and lost on other men's ambitions, and a captain who can hold what the ironborn already have instead of drowning more of them chasing more. It loses to a bolder promise. Euron arrives mid-vote, announces he is the one who killed Balon, and offers the captains conquest instead of restraint; the salt-and-rock throne goes to him. Yara does not stay to contest it twice — she and Theon take the fastest ships in the fleet before Euron can stop them and sail for Meereen, where she offers Daenerys Targaryen ships and men in exchange for a crown that answers to no one but the Iron Islands itself."],
    7: ["Her fleet, carrying Ellaria Sand and Oberyn's daughters north to seal the Dornish alliance, sails into her uncle's ambush in open water, and the fight that follows is not close. Euron cuts down two of the Sand Snakes in the boarding, takes Ellaria and the third alive, and corners Yara herself; Theon, frozen by the same paralysis that has followed him since Winterfell, goes over the side instead of to her aid, and she is dragged off in chains while her own men watch him swim away."],
    8: ["Held on Euron's ships through the winter, and freed only when Theon finally does what he could not do at sea and comes back for her with a knife. She sends him south to die at Winterfell settling his own accounts and sails home to settle hers, retaking the Iron Islands in his place. She is at the council in the ruins of King's Landing when the realm's lords choose their next ruler, and leaves it with the one thing she came for: the Iron Islands, hers, answering to no crown but its own."],
  },

  "Euron Greyjoy": {
    6: ["Arrives at his brother's Kingsmoot mid-vote, unmourning, and announces without much ceremony that he is the one who killed Balon — a confession delivered like a boast, to a crowd that finds it more persuasive than threatening. He out-promises his niece by promising more: not restraint but conquest, a fleet given to Daenerys Targaryen along with a marriage proposal, dragons brought home to finish what Balon's rebellion never could. The captains salute him for it. His own niece and nephew rob him of the ships before he can sail anywhere, stealing the fastest hulls in the fleet out from under his new crown, and he is left to build another one and swear he will finish what he started."],
    7: ["Makes good on half his promise the hard way: not Daenerys, but a fleet built fast enough to catch his niece's at sea and break it, taking Ellaria and Tyene Sand prisoner and delivering them to Cersei Lannister as a gift he was never asked for. Cersei names him master of ships for it, and he proposes marriage in the same breezy register he uses for everything, undeterred by her lack of an answer. When the war council at King's Landing turns to fighting the army of the dead, Euron sails off rather than commit his fleet to it, telling Cersei plainly that he has no intention of dying for a corpse."],
    8: ["Returns to King's Landing with the Golden Company in tow, making good, finally, on the one promise he never broke — not dragons, but sellswords bought with Lannister gold while everyone assumed he had simply run. He collects on the marriage he proposed the year before, at least for as long as it amuses Cersei and irritates her brother, which seems to be most of what he wants from it. He mans a scorpion himself against Dany's fleet in the crossing and puts bolt after bolt into Rhaegal until the dragon goes down over open water, the cleanest blow anyone lands on a dragon all series. His own fleet is burned to nothing when Daenerys turns her last dragon on the city, and he crawls from the wreckage to find Jaime Lannister waiting for him — the fight he has wanted since Jaime took his place beside Cersei. He wounds Jaime badly enough to die believing he has killed him; it is close enough to true that no one bothers to correct him."],
  },

  "Walder Frey": {
    1: ["Lord of the Crossing, and he never lets anyone forget what that phrase is worth. Robb Stark needs the Twins to move his army north of the Trident, and Walder Frey makes him pay for it in the coin he values most — not gold, but obligation: safe passage and swords in exchange for a marriage pact, Robb's own hand promised to one of Frey's numerous daughters once the war is won. He extracts the promise slowly, relishing the King in the North's need of him, and lets Robb cross the river knowing exactly how much leverage he has just banked."],
    3: ["Collects on a debt Robb Stark never intended to pay in full. The marriage broken, the insult worn for a season, Frey accepts amends in a different shape — Edmure Tully in his daughter Roslin's bed instead of Robb in one of his — and hosts the wedding at the Twins with every appearance of a grudge finally settled. The music, the wine, and the goodwill last exactly as long as it takes his men to bar the doors. He gives the signal himself, calm as a man closing a ledger, and the Starks he has fed and toasted all evening die in his hall before the night is out."],
    6: ["Killed in his own castle years later, at the hands of a girl he never recognized wearing a face that was not her own — the debt for the Twins called in at last, long after he thought he had closed the account for good."],
  },

  "Edmure Tully": {
    1: ["Heir to Riverrun and eager to prove it, first by ambushing Jaime Lannister's forces at the Golden Tooth and winning a skirmish that flatters his instincts more than it tests his judgment. Riverrun braces for the wider war while his father Hoster lies dying upstairs, and Edmure holds the seat in practice before he holds it in name, restless to be tested in the field and not yet sure what the test will actually cost him."],
    3: ["Buries his father on the river the old Tully way, and cannot even manage the arrow — three shots into the water before the flaming bolt finally reaches the boat, and it is Brynden, not Edmure, who ends the embarrassment with one clean shot of his own. Then he is married off to Roslin Frey in payment for Robb's broken promise, a match he has no say in and no particular objection to, right up until the doors of the Twins are barred behind him and his own wedding turns into the massacre that ends his family's war."],
    6: ["Held prisoner since the Red Wedding and finally worth something to his captors again: brought to the walls of Riverrun in a Bolton-Frey column, the price of the castle's surrender written on his face for his uncle to read. Jaime Lannister lets him choose the shape of his own ruin, and Edmure, worn down past defiance, walks in and orders the gates opened himself, handing Riverrun to the Freys without a siege and undoing everything Brynden spent the season holding."],
    8: ["Turns up at Winterfell in time for the war council before the dead arrive, still finding his footing among lords who have spent years fighting battles he only heard about as a prisoner, and volunteers Riverrun's strength before anyone has quite finished asking. It is a smaller part than the war deserves of him, and he plays it gamely."],
  },

  "Brynden Tully": {
    1: ["The steadiest judgment in the Tully family, and the one Robb Stark's war can least afford to lose. With Hoster dying and Edmure impatient for a fight he is not yet ready to win, Brynden holds Riverrun's defense together by sheer experience, counsels caution the way a man does who has already seen where recklessness leads, and earns from his family the kind of trust his brash nephew has not yet learned to ask for."],
    3: ["Sees his brother Hoster buried on the river with no one able to light the arrow but him, after Edmure's three failed shots turn a solemn rite into something harder to watch. He survives the Red Wedding by the thinnest of margins — out of the hall relieving himself when the doors are barred and the music turns to slaughter — and rides out of the ruin of his family's alliance alone, one of the only Tullys or Starks to leave the Twins breathing."],
    6: ["Turns up alive after years unaccounted for, having retaken Riverrun with a scratch force while the rest of Westeros assumed him dead, and holds it against a Frey-Bolton siege with the same flat competence he has always brought to a losing hand. Brienne comes asking him to bring his men north to Sansa's cause; he will not abandon the castle to do it, choosing the last Tully seat over an alliance he cannot be sure will hold. When Edmure is forced to order the gates opened, Brynden refuses to leave with the surrender, and dies inside Riverrun rather than yield it walking."],
  },

  "Loras Tyrell": {
    2: ["Renly Baratheon's closest companion and, in the private language the camps use, something more — sworn to the Rainbow Guard and rarely far from the king's tent. He works the political side of the alliance as fluently as Renly works a room, pressing his sister's claim on the crown through marriage rather than swords wherever swords can be avoided. When a shadow with Stannis's face kills Renly in his own tent, Loras is the one who finds him, and the grief comes out first as an accusation: he goes for Brienne with a blade before anyone can explain what actually happened, and it takes Catelyn Stark's word to stop him from killing the one person who might have avenged the man he loved."],
    3: ["The Tyrells hedge their bet and move Loras onto the board as a husband instead of a mourner. Betrothed briefly to Sansa Stark in a plan Olenna assembles to put a Tyrell claim on the North before anyone else thinks of it, he loses her the moment Tywin Lannister notices the same opening and claims Sansa for Tyrion instead. Reassigned to Cersei, he receives the news of his own engagement with the enthusiasm of a man being sentenced, and does little to hide it. Around King's Landing he keeps company with his squire Olyvar, a friendship the city gossips about more freely than Loras seems to want, and the deflection he offers Sansa when she asks about his tastes is polite, practiced, and not remotely convincing."],
    4: ["Still promised to Cersei and no happier about it than before, Loras spends Joffrey's wedding as a groom-in-waiting rather than a guest of honor, standing near a throne his family helped buy for a king he cannot stand. When Joffrey collapses and dies at his own feast, the shock in the hall does not touch the Tyrell table for long; a son betrothed to the dead king's mother is still a useful thing to be, whatever anyone privately felt about the boy. Loras says little and is asked little, which suits him fine."],
    5: ["The Faith Militant plot: Cersei restores the Sparrows and turns them loose on the Tyrells, and Loras is the first target. Arrested for his relationship with Olyvar — and for the men before him — he is dragged into a cell as a 'sexual deviant' while Cersei watches the family that has been crowding her for two seasons take the blow she engineered. Margaery lies under oath to protect him and is arrested herself for the perjury, which leaves brother and sister in adjoining cells at the mercy of a High Sparrow neither of them can bribe or charm. Loras spends the season's end exactly where Cersei wanted him — not dead, not free, and no use to anyone."],
    6: ["Imprisoned through most of the year and visibly broken by it, he reappears thin, cowed, and missing an ear, ready to confess to whatever the Faith wants confessed and to give up his name along with his sins. He walks the walk of atonement at Margaery's side, shorn and silent, and means to take vows and hand Highgarden to the Faith entirely rather than fight for it — the Tyrell heir surrendering the house his grandmother has spent two seasons trying to save. He never gets the chance: the Sept of Baelor goes up in wildfire with the trial still underway, and Loras dies in the blast along with the family he was about to renounce and the Faith he had just joined."],
  },

  "Mace Tyrell": {
    3: ["Lord of Highgarden, and considerably easier to read than his mother — a broad, genial man who says the quiet part out loud about a marriage alliance everyone else is being subtle about. He arrives in King's Landing to finalize Margaery's match to Joffrey, delighted by the idea of a Tyrell queen and apparently untroubled by what kind of king Joffrey is. He talks trade and gold with Tywin, foots bills the crown cannot cover, and generally serves as proof that House Tyrell's wealth, rather than its wits, is what bought this family a seat at the table — the wits, everyone in the room understands, belong to Olenna."],
    4: ["Present for Joffrey's wedding as the beaming father of the bride, more invested in the spectacle than in the groom, and just as blindsided as everyone else when Joffrey dies mid-toast. The match he spent a year building collapses in front of him, and he pivots without much visible grief toward the obvious replacement — Tommen is younger, more pliable, and still a king, and a Tyrell-Lannister wedding is a Tyrell-Lannister wedding whichever brother is standing at the altar. Mace's contribution to the family's schemes, as ever, is money and enthusiasm rather than subtlety, and he leaves the plotting to his mother and daughter without seeming to notice he has been sidelined."],
    5: ["Cersei, tired of Tyrell money and Tyrell leverage cluttering up her court, finds an elegant way to remove Mace from the board entirely: she sends him to Braavos to negotiate with the Iron Bank, a task that sounds prestigious and keeps him thousands of miles from King's Landing while she goes to work on his children. Mace accepts the honor happily, entirely missing that it is an exile dressed up as an errand, and spends the season at sea and in foreign counting-houses while Margaery is arrested and Loras is thrown in a cell back home."],
    6: ["Mace returns to a King's Landing he barely recognizes — his son mutilated and cowed, his daughter humiliated in the street, his mother furious and sidelined, and the crown he helped buy now openly hostile to his family. He has no plan beyond standing near his children as they face the Faith's judgment, which is the only role left to him by this point. He is among the nobles and Tyrells gathered at the Sept of Baelor for Loras and Cersei's trial, and when the wildfire takes the building down around them, Mace goes with it — no scene, no last word, just one more name added to a very long list that day."],
  },

  "Oberyn Martell": {
    4: ["Arrives in King's Landing for Joffrey's wedding with Ellaria on his arm and an old grievance he has never let go of: his sister Elia was raped and murdered during the Sack of King's Landing, and the man who did it, Gregor Clegane, still serves the family Oberyn has come to visit. He needles Tywin and Cersei about it without much subtlety, samples the city's brothels and poisons with equal expertise, and takes an interest in Tyrion's murder trial mostly because the man in the dock is a Lannister he does not hate. When Tyrion demands a trial by combat, Oberyn volunteers as his champion — not out of loyalty, but because it is finally a legal way to put a spear through Gregor Clegane. He fights brilliantly, gets his confession, and then lingers over it, demanding Gregor say Elia's name clearly instead of finishing him — and it costs him his skull in front of the entire court."],
  },

  "Ellaria Sand": {
    4: ["Oberyn's paramour and, by her own account, happier being his lover than being anyone's wife — she comes to King's Landing with him for Joffrey's wedding and stays close through the brothel scenes and the small council needling, plainly enjoying how much discomfort the two of them cause the Lannisters just by being unimpressed. She backs his obsession with Elia without ever quite talking him out of the parts of it that will get him killed, and when he volunteers to fight Gregor Clegane she is there to watch, certain he will win right up until the moment he does not. His death leaves her in the crowd, holding nothing, while the vengeance the whole trip was supposed to deliver goes up in a spray of blood that is entirely his own."],
    5: ["Grief has curdled into something harder by the time Dorne reopens the story: Ellaria wants the Lannisters to bleed for Oberyn and has no patience left for Doran's decades of careful peace-keeping. She falls in with Oberyn's daughters, the Sand Snakes, and the group of them work against their own prince as much as against King's Landing, furious that Myrcella Baratheon gets to live safely in Dorne as a hostage-turned-guest while Oberyn rots in the ground. It is Ellaria who finally gets close enough to do something about it, kissing Myrcella goodbye at the docks with poison on her lips — a slow-acting revenge dressed up as tenderness, set loose just as the girl's ship pulls away."],
    6: ["Ellaria stops waiting on Doran's patience and ends it herself, working with the Sand Snakes to kill both the prince and his son Trystane within the same night — Doran in his own chambers, Trystane on the ship still carrying him home. She takes Dorne for her own with the Sand Snakes at her side, and by the end of the season has traded her old grievance against the crown for a seat in a much bigger conspiracy, offering Dorne's spears and ships to the alliance gathering behind Daenerys Targaryen."],
    7: ["Dorne throws its fleet in with the grand alliance sailing for Westeros, and Ellaria sails with it, her daughter Tyene at her side and old scores apparently settled. Euron Greyjoy has other plans: his fleet catches the Dornish ships at sea, and Ellaria and Tyene are taken alive and delivered to Cersei as payback for Myrcella dressed up as a gift. Cersei returns the favor from two seasons back with interest, poisoning Tyene with the same slow venom Ellaria used on Myrcella and chaining Ellaria in a cell to watch her daughter die by inches, close enough to talk to and powerless to help. It is the same cruelty Ellaria dealt out at the docks, aimed back at her exactly once and left to work."],
  },

  "Doran Martell": {
    5: ["Prince of Dorne, gout-ridden and wheelchair-bound, and the opposite temperament from his dead brother in every way that matters — Doran wants peace with the Lannisters, not payback, and has spent years quietly keeping that promise instead of shouting about it. He handles the mess Jaime and Bronn's covert mission drops in his lap by doing the sensible thing nobody around him wants: he lets Jaime take Myrcella home, arranges a marriage between his son Trystane and the girl instead, and refuses Ellaria's demands for war outright, going so far as to have the Sand Snakes locked up when they try to force his hand. It costs him what little goodwill he has left in his own household, and it does not even work — Myrcella sails north with poison already working in her, a parting gift from Ellaria that Doran never sees coming."],
  },

  "Barristan Selmy": {
    1: ["Lord Commander of the Kingsguard and the last of Robert's circle who still looks the part — old, plainly built, and visibly bored by a peacetime court that has no use for a soldier's skills. He talks jousting with Ned Stark at the Hand's tournament with real longing, a man who would rather be unhorsed in the lists than stand guard over squabbling lords, and is otherwise exactly where a Lord Commander should be: silent, armored, and background to everyone else's plot."],
    3: ["Joffrey decides an old knight is a decorative knight and strips Barristan of his white cloak in open court, replacing him with a returned, one-handed Jaime Lannister and delivering the news with the same casual cruelty he hands out everything else. It is meant as a humiliation and lands as one, but Barristan does not slink off to die quietly the way Joffrey clearly expects; a lifetime of service just got taken from him for nothing, and he leaves King's Landing looking for a cause that will actually value it."],
    4: ["That cause turns out to be Daenerys Targaryen. Barristan has been traveling under the name Arstan Whitebeard, working alongside Jorah Mormont as a supposed old family retainer, close enough to the queen to protect her before she even knows who he really is. When his identity comes out, so does Jorah's — years ago he sent reports on Daenerys to Robert's court, a betrayal that should be a death sentence, and it is Barristan who argues for exile over execution, though Daenerys decides it for herself either way. He settles into Meereen as her sworn shield and one of the only advisors old enough, and honest enough, to tell her when she is wrong."],
    5: ["Daenerys' longest-serving conscience: he pushes back on her harder justice with the patience of a man who served three kings and watched all three go wrong in different ways, arguing for mercy in the matter of the Meereenese nobles' sons and for restraint generally, not always successfully. When the Sons of the Harpy ambush the streets, Barristan goes out with Grey Worm to protect Meereen's people rather than waiting inside the pyramid, and the old knight who once complained about a peacetime court finally gets the fight he always said he wanted. He goes down surrounded, blade still moving, having spent his whole final battle exactly the way he told Ned Stark years earlier he wished he could."],
  },

  "Jeor Mormont": {
    1: ["Lord Commander of an order that used to matter and now scrapes by on criminals, poachers, and boys nobody wanted, and he knows exactly what he has and what he needs. He notices Jon Snow's aptitude before Jon does himself, and makes him steward over Jon's furious objections — not as a reward, he explains, but because a commander needs someone reading over his shoulder. He receives Benjen Stark's failure to return beyond the Wall as the crisis it is and starts planning a ranging in force. A wight gets into his own chambers at Castle Black in the dead of night, and it is Jon who saves his life, a debt Mormont does not intend to leave unpaid."],
    2: ["He pays that debt at the start of the ranging, giving Jon his family's Valyrian steel sword rather than let it rust in a chest for a son who has disowned him. He leads several hundred men beyond the Wall — the largest muster the Watch has fielded in a generation — hunting for Benjen Stark and answers about the things Craster whispers of in the deep snow. At the Fist of the First Men he makes camp and waits, aware he is stretching his men past what the Watch was ever built to survive, and sends Jon out scouting with Qhorin Halfhand because he trusts Qhorin's judgment more than his own optimism. The wait ends with the dead coming over the ridge."],
    3: ["The retreat from the Fist becomes a death march, and by the time the survivors reach Craster's Keep his authority is worth less than the last of the food. He holds the line on discipline anyway — no thieving, no killing the man whose roof they are sheltering under — because it is the only thing he still has to give them, and the men who have started calling him an old fool in low voices do not want to hear it. Karl Tanner and the others rise up at Craster's table with knives instead of arguments; Mormont goes for his sword too late, and dies telling men he has commanded for years exactly what they have become."],
  },

  "Maester Aemon": {
    1: ["Blind, past ninety, and content to be thought of as just the old man who tends the ravens, though everyone at Castle Black who bothers to ask knows Aemon Targaryen chose the Wall over a throne two civil wars ago. He takes an interest in Jon Snow that has nothing to do with sympathy for another man's bastard and everything to do with recognizing the particular loneliness of a boy who has never fully belonged anywhere. When word reaches the Wall that Ned Stark is dead and Robb has called the banners, Aemon is the one who talks Jon out of riding south, laying out — gently, and from experience — exactly what it costs a man to choose duty over blood."],
    3: ["Castle Black spends the season waiting on news from beyond the Wall, most of it bad, until Sam Tarly staggers up to the gate in the last hours of the year with Gilly and Craster's infant son and nothing left in him to argue with a guard. Aemon is the one who cuts through the Watch's centuries-old rule against harboring wildlings and orders the gate opened without needing to see who is standing on the other side of it, and it is Aemon, not the men with working eyes, who understands first exactly what has just arrived at the Wall."],
    4: ["With most of the Watch's fighting strength stripped for the Wall's defense, Aemon becomes the closest thing Castle Black has to a war council chief who never leaves his chair. He dictates ravens sent out to every keep and holdfast in the Seven Kingdoms warning that the wildling army is coming and the Wall will not hold itself, aware from three hundred years of the Watch's history exactly how many of those letters will actually be answered. When the attack comes he stays where he is most useful, out of the fighting and close to a bell that needs ringing, ancient and steady while younger men lose their nerve around him."],
    5: ["Jon sends him south with Sam and Gilly and the baby, ostensibly so Sam can begin training at the Citadel, mostly because Aemon is too old and Castle Black too dangerous for him to stay any longer. A storm blows their ship off course to Braavos instead of Oldtown, and it is there, in a stranger's house, that his age finally catches him. He dies with Sam and Gilly beside him, drifting back to a boyhood nobody at the Wall ever knew he had, mistaking the moment for a much earlier one and calling for a brother the rest of the world remembers only as a king."],
  },

  "Alliser Thorne": {
    1: ["Master-at-arms at Castle Black and about as warm a welcome as the Wall has to offer: a knight with real battlefield credit behind him, reduced now to beating discipline into thieves and poachers, and he does not let any of them forget what he thinks of the job. He rides Jon Snow hardest of all the new recruits, resents the boy's swordsmanship and his highborn manners in equal measure, and loses the yard fight badly enough to make it personal. When Mormont needs proof of what came over the Wall carried south to a court that will not want to hear it, he sends Thorne with the wight's severed hand — a punishment dressed up as a mission, and Thorne knows it."],
    3: ["He comes back from King's Landing to find the Watch he left in worse shape than the one that sent him away, and Castle Black very obviously being run in his absence by men he does not respect. Jon Snow returns from beyond the Wall having lived and fought and slept among wildlings, and Thorne says out loud what plenty of others are only thinking: that a man does not come back from that unmarked, and the Watch would be foolish to just take his word for which side he is actually on. He pushes for Jon to be tried as a deserter and spy rather than welcomed home, and loses that argument, which does nothing to change his mind."],
    4: ["With Mormont dead and no Lord Commander elected, Thorne is one of the men effectively running Castle Black, and he runs it the way he has always wanted to — hard, humorless, and suspicious of Jon Snow's judgment on principle. When Mance Rayder's army finally reaches the Wall he fights the siege seriously, holding the gate and the top of the ice with men he has spent years training, whatever else is said about him. Stannis Baratheon's cavalry breaks the wildling army at the last possible moment, and Thorne survives the battle that killed better-liked men than him, which he seems to take as vindication rather than luck."],
    5: ["He stands against Jon Snow for Lord Commander and loses a close vote, and takes the defeat the way he takes everything — as one more piece of evidence that the Watch has lost its sense. Jon's decisions once in command only confirm it as far as Thorne is concerned: bringing wildlings south of the Wall to live as neighbors instead of enemies, arming the same people who spent the season trying to kill them all at Hardhome. Thorne says nothing shows the rot more clearly, and he is not alone in saying it. He, Bowen Marsh, and a circle of officers who have run out of patience corner Jon in the courtyard at Castle Black and each put a knife in him, telling him it is for the Watch as he dies."],
    6: ["He holds Castle Black in the days after the mutiny, tells the men who still trust him that it was necessary and that the Watch is better for it, and does not flinch when Sansa Stark's letters and Tormund's wildlings start pressing at the gates from outside. When Jon Snow comes back from the dead and the castle will not fight for a ghost, Thorne surrenders it without much of a struggle, unwilling to burn Castle Black down over a battle he already knows he has lost. He is hanged for the mutiny in the yard alongside the other ringleaders and the boy who fired the last arrow, and goes to it without protesting his innocence or begging — the one thing about him nobody watching can find fault with."],
  },

  "Janos Slynt": {
    1: ["Commander of the City Watch of King's Landing, which makes him a man with two thousand gold cloaks and no loyalty attached to any of them beyond whoever is currently signing his pay. Ned Stark believes those cloaks are his to command when he moves against the Lannisters; Slynt has already sold them to Petyr Baelish and Cersei before Ned finishes explaining the plan, and turns his men on Ned's household guard the moment it matters. He is rewarded with a lordship and Harrenhal for it, a castle everyone else in Westeros knows better than to want, and accepts both with the satisfaction of a man who thinks he has finally been recognized for his talents."],
    2: ["Tyrion Lannister arrives as acting Hand with a long memory for who did what during the coup, and Slynt is the first name he crosses off the list of men worth keeping around. Stripped of his new lordship in the same breath it takes to pronounce it, protesting the whole way down the stairs about his years of loyal service, he is bundled onto a ship north before he can finish the sentence. He arrives at Castle Black a disgraced lord in a black cloak among boys half his age, still telling anyone who will listen that this is a mistake that will be corrected as soon as the right people hear about it."],
    5: ["Years at the Wall have not made him any less certain that rank is owed to men like him rather than earned, and he treats Jon Snow's election as Lord Commander as an outrage to be waited out rather than an order to be followed. When Jon commands him to lead a company back to Craster's Keep to deal with the mutineers holed up there, Slynt refuses to his face in front of the assembled Watch, gambling that a green nineteen-year-old will not enforce it. He is wrong, and finds out how wrong at the block a few minutes later, marched there by men who have stopped finding his excuses interesting — the last and smallest use anyone gets out of Janos Slynt's lifelong belief that someone else would always clean up after him."],
  },

  "Mance Rayder": {
    3: ["King-Beyond-the-Wall by choice of the free folk rather than birth, and the only man in the story who deserted the Night's Watch and made something bigger out of it than the life he left behind. He has spent years doing what no one before him managed — talking a hundred warring clans into one army — for a single unglamorous reason: the White Walkers are coming, and the only way through matters more than pride about how it is done, over the Wall or around it, before winter shuts the door. When his riders bring him a captured Night's Watch deserter claiming to have killed one of his own officers to prove himself, Mance sees through the story almost immediately and lets Jon Snow live anyway, curious what a boy raised on the Watch's version of the world will do once he is inside a different one."],
    4: ["He commits everything to one push at the Wall — giants, mammoths, a hundred ladders going up the ice at once, thousands of fighters he has spent years assembling for exactly this — and the plan almost works. Castle Black holds by the width of a bell tower and dumb luck, and rather than throw away more of his people on a wall that will not fall tonight, Mance agrees to meet Jon Snow south of it and talk instead of fight, willing to let the whole campaign end in a negotiation if it saves the free folk he brought this far. Stannis Baratheon's cavalry arrives in the middle of the conversation and ends the debate for both of them, and Mance is taken prisoner, offered his life for a bent knee he will not give even with the fire already being built."],
  },

  "Ygritte": {
    2: ["One of Qhorin Halfhand's wildling captives turns out to be nobody's captive for long: Ygritte survives the ambush that kills the rest of her raiding party, and Jon Snow is given the job of executing her alone in the woods, blade to a kneeling woman's neck, and cannot make himself do it. What was supposed to take five minutes turns into days of the two of them alone in the haunted forest, Jon trying to march her back to the Wall as a prisoner and Ygritte making it very clear who actually has the upper hand between them, needling him with the same four words until they stick: you know nothing, Jon Snow. She leads him — deliberately or not, she never quite says — straight back into the hands of Mance Rayder's army."],
    3: ["She gets exactly what she wants out of the season: Jon Snow turned wildling in earnest, sharing her furs and her cause and, for one night in a cave behind a waterfall, her bed, no crows or oaths standing between them. She teaches him what the free folk actually are — not monsters, just people locked out of the only good land left by a wall and a war they never started — and half believes she has made a convert. When Jon breaks during the raid on the farm, refusing to help kill an old man who never did any of them harm, she reads it correctly before anyone else does. He runs; she puts three arrows in his back rather than let him go clean, and then, with a clear shot at the fourth, does not take it."],
    4: ["She marches on Castle Black with Tormund's raiding party still furious at Jon Snow and still, underneath it, not entirely able to hate him the way the betrayal should warrant. In the chaos of the assault she has him at her mercy again, this time with nothing stopping her, and hesitates exactly long enough for a boy named Olly to put an arrow through her from the wall above. She dies in Jon's arms by the fire, in the room where they first slept together, asking only to stay a little longer in the cave they never should have left, and telling him, for the last time and without the sting she used to put in it, that he still knows nothing."],
  },

  "Gendry": {
    1: ["A blacksmith's apprentice in King's Landing with a face that keeps getting him a second look from strangers he's never met, and a bull's-head helm he made himself that he won't sell no matter what's offered. Ned Stark comes to his forge asking about a sword and leaves looking rattled; neither of them says why. When the Watch recruiter Yoren comes through Flea Bottom short on volunteers, Gendry signs on more to get out of the city than out of any love for the Wall, unaware that a queen would rather he didn't exist at all. He heads north with Yoren's ragged column of thieves and orphans, one of whom is a Stark girl traveling as a boy."],
    2: ["North with Yoren's recruits until Lannister soldiers under Ser Amory Lorch run them down; Yoren dies covering the retreat, and the survivors are marched to Harrenhal as prisoners. A man they call the Tickler works down the line asking after gold and outlaws and killing for the wrong answer, and when it's Gendry's turn, Arya blurts out that he's highborn to make him worth more alive than dead — a lie built on the one fact about him she doesn't know is true. Tywin Lannister takes the castle in hand soon after and puts the torturing away as bad economics; he admires the quality of a horseshoe Gendry has made without ever learning whose son struck it. Gendry works the forge the rest of the season, plotting with Arya to slip away."],
    3: ["Sold by the Brotherhood to Melisandre for gold they need more than they need one more sword — a betrayal Arya never quite forgives them, or herself, for missing. At Dragonstone the red priestess tells him whose son he actually is and puts leeches to his skin, murmuring the names of three rival kings over the blood she draws, while Stannis watches with the patience of a man finally calling in a debt he's sure the universe owes him. When leeches aren't blood enough and the fire is made ready for him instead, Davos Seaworth — who can't read half of what crosses his desk but can still tell right from wrong — breaks him out of his cell and puts him in a rowboat pointed at nowhere in particular, on the theory that alive and lost beats efficient and dead."],
    7: ["Found in a Flea Bottom forge by Davos, who remembers an old debt and repays it by getting him out of King's Landing a second time, into Jon Snow's company beyond the Wall. Issued a war hammer instead of a sword, which suits him better anyway, he's the one who volunteers to run for help when the ranging party is trapped on a frozen lake surrounded by the dead — a proposal everyone laughs at until he actually does it, covering the distance to Eastwatch fast enough to get a raven off to Daenerys in time. He is waiting on the ice when the dragons arrive, in time to watch one of them not fly home again."],
    8: ["Forging dragonglass weapons through the nights before the Battle of Winterfell as fast as the smiths can heat the ore, arming half the castle himself. He survives the battle, and Arya comes to his forge the night before it to spend what might be their last hours as something other than friends. Afterward, at Winterfell's high table, Daenerys names him legitimate — Gendry Baratheon, Lord of Storm's End, the first acknowledgment his father's family has ever given him — and he immediately asks Arya to be his lady, getting the honest no he probably should have expected from a woman who spent years learning to want nothing that comes with a title. He turns up months later at the council in King's Landing anyway, one more lord with a say in who sits the throne that used to belong to his father."],
  },

  "Podrick Payne": {
    2: ["Assigned to Tyrion as squire by Tywin, who means it as an insult — a Payne of the disgraced, landless branch of the family, about as far from an honor as the name can manage. He turns out to be quietly, almost accidentally excellent at the job: attentive, unbothered by Tyrion's tongue, and present with a spear at exactly the moment a Lannister knight is about to finish off his master on the riverbank during the Battle of the Blackwater. He never tells anyone how he did it, which somehow makes it more impressive rather than less."],
    3: ["Tyrion tries to reward him for Blackwater with a night — several nights — with the finest brothel King's Landing has to offer, and is baffled to hear the girls refuse payment afterward, unable to get a straight explanation out of Podrick as to what exactly he did to earn that. He keeps close through Tyrion's forced betrothal to Sansa Stark and the wedding that follows, pouring wine and saying nothing, which by now everyone understands is simply how he shows up for people."],
    4: ["At Joffrey's wedding when the king dies choking at his own feast, and one of the few people who still wants to help Tyrion once the whole capital decides he did it. He digs for witnesses to build a defense and comes back with black eyes and no answers, having clearly been warned off by people with more power than he has. Tyrion, out of gratitude and dread in roughly equal measure, refuses to let him testify or dig further and sends him out of King's Landing entirely with nothing but a letter and a horse, buying his safety by putting him beyond anyone's reach."],
    5: ["Squire to Brienne of Tarth now, passed into her care on Tyrion's word, an odd pairing by every outward measure that nonetheless works. They track Sansa to the woods outside Winterfell and keep a cold, patient watch for the one signal Brienne was promised — a candle lit in the highest window — while Podrick spends the season mostly cooking, keeping watch, and getting steadily better with a sword under Brienne's repetitive, unglamorous instruction. The candle finally appears, but only after Brienne has ridden off to deal with a dying Stannis, and they miss their chance to reach Sansa that year."],
    6: ["At Riverrun with Brienne, sent to talk the Blackfish into lending his remaining men to Sansa's cause, before the negotiations run into a complication neither of them expected: Jaime Lannister, commanding the besieging army on the other side of the walls. Podrick watches the two of them work through a history he only half understands and gets nothing for the effort — the Blackfish refuses to bend, and Brienne and Pod leave by boat before the castle falls, still bound for Winterfell and Sansa with empty hands and Jaime's continued goodwill instead."],
    7: ["Drilling Winterfell's green recruits at Brienne's side, taking hit after hit from men twice his size with a patience that slowly starts looking like its own kind of skill, while Tormund Giantsbane hangs around the training yard making no secret of who he's really there to watch. Podrick travels south with Brienne as part of the party bringing Jon and Tyrion's fragile truce to King's Landing, one more sworn sword standing quietly at the edge of a room where dragons and queens are doing all the talking."],
    8: ["Sings 'Jenny of Oldstones' around the fire the night before the dead arrive at Winterfell, one small unguarded moment from someone who has spent years being nothing but reliable, and then survives the battle itself to be teased about the singing afterward. He remains at Brienne's side through the last uneasy months of the war, one more quiet constant in a story that has, by now, killed off almost everyone flashier than he is."],
  },

  "Qyburn": {
    3: ["Introduced tending the raw stump where Jaime Lannister's hand used to be, with a bedside manner built entirely around competence rather than comfort. He was a maester once, chain stripped from his neck by the Citadel for experiments they preferred not to describe in detail, and he has clearly not stopped experimenting since. He patches Brienne up after the bear pit at Harrenhal without asking the questions a more conventional physician might, and by the time the party reaches King's Landing he has quietly made himself useful enough to Cersei that no one thinks to ask why a disgraced maester is still hanging around the Red Keep."],
    4: ["Settled into the Red Keep as a kind of freelance physician to the Lannisters, useful precisely because he has no chain left to answer to and no colleagues left to disapprove. When Gregor Clegane takes a poisoned spear through the trial by combat that decides Tyrion's fate and lies dying of it in agony, Cersei brings the problem to Qyburn rather than to any maester with scruples still intact, and tells him to do whatever the work requires. He accepts without asking what 'whatever' will end up meaning, which is exactly why she picked him."],
    5: ["Unveils what 'whatever it takes' turned out to mean: a silent, towering thing in Kingsguard armor that used to be Gregor Clegane and now answers only to Cersei, presented to the court as Ser Robert Strong without further explanation invited. With Varys gone east and the small council short a set of ears, Cersei hands Qyburn the spy network's remains and lets him rebuild it his own way, recruiting the city's homeless children as informants because they go unnoticed and cost almost nothing. When the Faith Militant throw Cersei in a cell, it is Qyburn, not any lord, who keeps working her release from outside the walls."],
    6: ["Names Ser Robert Strong as Cersei's champion for the trial the High Sparrow has scheduled for her, a fight that never happens because Cersei solves the problem with wildfire instead of a swordsman. When the Sept of Baelor goes up and takes the Faith and half her enemies with it, Qyburn's little birds have already done their share of the groundwork, tracking who needed tracking and reporting back without ever being seen. Cersei rewards him with the small council seat outright, Master of Whisperers in title as well as function, the first time in his life anyone has handed him an honor instead of taking one away."],
    7: ["Master of Whisperers now, with a war against dragons to help plan for, which calls for tools no maester ever devised. He designs an enormous crossbow capable of driving a bolt through a dragon's eye, and gets close enough to proving it works when Bronn puts a bolt into Drogon's shoulder during Daenerys's attack on the Lannister loot train — not a kill, but closer than anyone else in the war manages. He stays close at Cersei's side through the parley in the Dragonpit, watching the Northern lords and the dragon queen agree to a peace he already assumes won't survive the winter."],
    8: ["Lines the coast and the fleet with scorpions and gets the kill this time — Rhaegal goes down into the sea on the approach to King's Landing, the single clean victory anyone manages against Daenerys's dragons in the whole war. It buys the city almost nothing; King's Landing falls anyway, and in the chaos of its burning Qyburn orders Ser Robert Strong to stay and guard Cersei rather than go looking for his brother. Gregor Clegane, who has never once in his life taken an order that mattered when a grudge was on offer, walks straight through Qyburn to get to Sandor, ending the only creature he ever fully controlled by crushing the skull of the man who built him."],
  },

  "Grand Maester Pycelle": {
    1: ["The oldest voice on the small council, and by Littlefinger's telling nowhere near as feeble as he plays at being — a long white beard doing a great deal of work to make every Hand who passes through underestimate him. He assures Ned Stark that Jon Arryn's death was natural and that there is nothing worth digging into in the matter of the king's bastards, reassurances that turn out, at best, to be incomplete. Whatever loyalty keeps him at that table has clearly never belonged to whichever Hand currently sits in the chair, and by the time Ned works that out, it has already cost him."],
    2: ["Caught by the oldest trick in Tyrion's admittedly short career as Hand: told a secret meant for him alone, in confidence, and watches it reach Cersei within the day. Tyrion has him dragged to the black cells and his famous beard shaved off him in front of witnesses, less for the treason itself than to strip away the costume of harmless old age he's been hiding behind for years. Without the beard he looks exactly as old, and exactly as sharp, as Littlefinger always said he was."],
    3: ["Restored to the small council the moment Tywin Lannister takes the Handship after Blackwater, beard regrown and grudges intact, though he is noticeably more careful now about which secrets he repeats and to whom. He resumes his old function — agreeing with whoever currently holds power, and reminiscing at length about King Aerys whenever anyone will let him — while giving no sign that months in a cell taught him anything except patience."],
    4: ["Summoned to Joffrey's side as the king chokes at his own wedding feast and pronounced dead by the only maester in the room, which puts Pycelle at the center of the investigation that follows without ever making him especially useful to it. He sits through Tyrion's trial offering the court his professional opinion that the boy king was poisoned, technically true and entirely unhelpful to a man he has disliked for years and is in no hurry to save."],
    5: ["Watches Cersei's regency for her son with the wary displeasure of a man who has outlasted several kings and does not rate her chances of outlasting this one. He grumbles about the High Sparrow's growing hold over the city and about Cersei's decision to arm the Faith as a private army, correctly predicting trouble while being far too easy to ignore for anyone to act on it. His warnings go into the small council record and nowhere else."],
    6: ["Finds himself edged off the council's real business by Qyburn, whose usefulness to Cersei has none of the moralizing Pycelle can't quite stop doing even now. Summoned to a council session that turns out to be a pretext, he is drawn away down a candlelit passage by a pack of Qyburn's little birds and killed there, quietly and efficiently, on Cersei's standing order to leave no one behind who remembers the old regime too fondly — one more loose end cut on the same night the Sept of Baelor goes up in wildfire and takes half his enemies with it."],
  },

  "Shae": {
    1: ["A camp follower Tyrion takes a liking to in the Riverlands and brings back to King's Landing with him, which is not remotely the kind of thing a Lannister is supposed to do out loud. She is installed as a handmaid to Sansa Stark for cover, a job she performs with more genuine warmth than the assignment requires, and the affection between her and Tyrion reads, against both their expectations, as something closer to real than either wants to call it."],
    2: ["Still playing lady's maid to Sansa while quietly running Tyrion's household and, increasingly, keeping Sansa upright through a year that keeps trying to break her. During the riot after Joffrey's name day and again through the terror of the Blackwater, Shae stays close and steady in a way no one is paying her to do, protective of a girl she was only ever supposed to be watching."],
    3: ["Undone by Tyrion's marriage to Sansa, which he insists is politics and nothing more, and which she experiences as exactly what it looks like — being quietly replaced by a wife he didn't choose either, in a house where nobody asked what she wanted. Tyrion tries to save her the only way he knows how, arranging passage to Pentos and a fortune to make the exile bearable, and she takes it as the rejection it partly is and refuses to go. Tywin finds her in his son's chambers near season's end and settles the matter himself, with jewels, a threat, and the flattest cruelty the old man has in him."],
    4: ["Takes the stand at Tyrion's trial for Joffrey's murder and tells the court a version of their life together designed to hurt him as badly as possible — calling him a monster, describing a conspiracy with Sansa that never happened — payment in kind for a betrayal she never stopped believing was real. Tyrion, loose in the Red Keep on his way out of the city for good, finds her in his father's bed instead of his own and strangles her with a chain before turning the crossbow meant for his escape on Tywin himself. It is the last thing he does in King's Landing, and neither Shae nor the father he shoots leaves that room alive."],
  },

  "Ros": {
    1: ["A prostitute at the brothel outside Winterfell where Theon Greyjoy is a regular customer and a worse tipper than his rank would suggest, one of the first people in the story to see exactly what Theon is when nobody important is watching. She turns up again in King's Landing working for Littlefinger, whose brothels function as half information exchange and half business, and who values a clever, observant woman for reasons that go well beyond the obvious ones."],
    2: ["Employed by Littlefinger and quietly informing on him to Varys at the same time, playing a game considerably more dangerous than the one either man thinks he's running. She helps get Shae settled into Sansa's household as a favor that costs her nothing and buys her some safety, or so it seems. When Joffrey takes his temper out on two of Littlefinger's girls with a crossbow for entertainment, Ros is one of them, and survives it with the kind of fear that doesn't go away just because the king moves on to something else."],
    3: ["Littlefinger works out, or decides it no longer matters whether he can prove, that she has been carrying his business to Varys, and rather than simply killing her himself he finds a use for the betrayal — handing her over to Joffrey as a gift. The king, who has never needed much of an excuse, kills her with a crossbow the way he tortured her with one the year before, and Littlefinger lets her body be found where Varys will hear about it, a message about what happens to people who work both sides of his table."],
  },

  "Beric Dondarrion": {
    3: ["Leader of the Brotherhood without Banners, and already a dead man several times over by the time Arya meets him — Thoros has pulled him back from the Lord of Light's fire again and again, and neither of them can fully explain why it keeps working. He puts Sandor Clegane on trial for the butcher's boy's murder and settles it the outlaw way, trial by combat, and loses: the Hound's sword finds him before his own burning blade does any good, and Thoros kneels over the body and prays him back once more, nobody bothering to count which time it is anymore. The cause needs gold more than it needs prisoners, so Gendry goes to Melisandre for a purse of gold dragons, over Arya's furious objections, and the Brotherhood rides on without him."],
    6: ["Years gone from the story and reappears exactly when the Hound needs him — Beric and Thoros arrive with the remnant of the Brotherhood in time to cut down the marauders who hanged the villagers Clegane had tried to help, the same men, more or less, who used to ride under Beric's own banner before they slipped off to plunder in his name. Over the fire afterward he tells Clegane plainly what he told Arya once and will tell others eventually: the Lord of Light keeps returning him for a purpose he still cannot name, and something is coming from beyond the Wall worth living to fight. He asks the Hound to ride north with them instead of chasing his own revenge, and the Hound, against his own habits, agrees."],
    7: ["North of the Wall with Jon Snow's ragged company to catch a wight and drag it south as proof — Beric, the Hound, Jorah, Tormund and the rest, walking into country that used to be a story wildlings told to frighten children. When the dead surround them on a frozen lake he's the one who lights his sword and holds the line, and the flame is the only thing keeping a wight bear off the Hound long enough to matter. Trapped through the night on a rock as the Night King's army closes in, he lives to see Daenerys arrive on dragonback to pull them out — and watches the Night King bring Viserion down out of the sky with a spear of ice, proof, if the chained wight wasn't proof enough, that the stories were never just stories."],
    8: ["Held over for one more purpose, and the Battle of Winterfell provides it. Cut off with the Hound and Arya in the castle's covered walkways as the dead pour through the breach, he throws himself at the wights again and again with nothing left in him but the fire on his sword, buying the seconds Arya needs to keep moving toward the godswood. It finally takes; the Lord of Light doesn't call him back this time, and the Hound, kneeling over him after, understands only then what all those resurrections were actually for — not Beric's war at all, but getting a girl with a dagger to the right room at the right hour."],
  },

  "Thoros of Myr": {
    3: ["Once the red priest attached to Robert Baratheon's court, more interested in the wine cellar than the Lord of Light, until the war he used to preach about arrived for real and left him stranded in the Riverlands with nothing left to hide behind. He has become the genuine article by the time Arya meets him — the man who pulls Beric back from death with prayers he still half-disbelieves even as they keep working, four times, five, he has lost the exact count. He officiates the trial by combat between Beric and the Hound with the weary competence of someone who has done this exact thing too many times, and when Beric falls, kneels over the body and does it again."],
    6: ["Turns up with Beric and what remains of the Brotherhood just as the Hound is about to be overrun by men who broke off from their own company to keep burning and hanging in Beric's name after he stopped sanctioning it — men Thoros plainly recognizes, and plainly has no patience left for. He sits by the fire afterward while Beric makes the pitch to Clegane about the Lord of Light and the war still coming, adding little beyond a flask and a shrug, a believer who stopped needing to sound like one a long time ago. When the Hound falls in with them, Thoros leads the company north toward the Wall, the flaming sword traded for the moment for a bottle passed hand to hand."],
    7: ["North of the Wall with Jon Snow's company to snatch a wight for proof, muttering about how a lifetime spent drinking Robert Baratheon's wine cellar dry never once prepared him for this. When a wight bear crashes into the camp in the dark, it gets him before anyone can burn it back — he goes down fighting, and this time there is no Lord of Light left standing over him to say the prayer. Beric buries him beyond the Wall, one resurrection short of his own, the debt finally called the other way after years of paying it out."],
  },

  "Daario Naharis": {
    3: ["One of three captains of the Second Sons, sent into Daenerys's camp outside Yunkai with Mero and Prendahl to talk terms or take her head, whichever pays better. He walks out again alone, the other two captains' heads in a sack, and lays the company's swords at her feet with a grin that suggests he'd have done it for free. Vain, quick with a knife, and entirely too pleased with his own cheekbones, he flirts his way through most of the siege that follows and helps her take Yunkai without much of a fight, since the city's slaves have their own reasons to open the gates first."],
    4: ["Second Sons folded into Daenerys's growing army now, and he throws himself into the plan to take Meereen with the same swagger he brought to Yunkai, working alongside Grey Worm to get men inside the walls before the slaves have to win their own freedom unaided. He becomes Daenerys's lover somewhere in the aftermath, a fact Ser Barristan disapproves of without ever quite saying so, and spends the rest of the season helping hold a newly conquered city that resents its liberators nearly as much as it resented its masters."],
    5: ["Meereen under him and Daenerys both, and neither the marriage alliance with Hizdahr nor the unrest in the streets leaves him much to do but hunt Sons of the Harpy through back alleys and bring back fewer answers than bodies. He fights in the pits alongside Daenerys's guard when the Harpy's men finally strike in the open, and stands with what's left of the court when Drogon carries her off over the walls. Left holding a city that no longer has its queen in it, he sets out with Jorah Mormont into the Dothraki Sea to find her, since waiting in Meereen for news has never suited him."],
    6: ["Finds Daenerys a captive of the Dothraki khals at Vaes Dothrak with Jorah, arrives to rescue her and discovers, as usual, that she has already solved the problem herself by burning the temple down around her captors. Escorts her back to a Meereen under siege by three slaver fleets, and watches her burn that problem too, from Drogon's back, while the city cheers. In council afterward she tells him plainly that he isn't sailing to Westeros with her — a foreign lover buys her no alliances there, and Meereen needs a steady hand more than her bed does — and leaves him behind with the Second Sons to hold the city, which is as close as Daario Naharis ever comes to being told no."],
  },

  "Jaqen H'ghar": {
    2: ["A prisoner in Yoren's cage bound for the Wall, chained between the killers Rorge and Biter, until the Lannister ambush at the crossroads burns the wagons and Arya pulls the pin on the cage door before she thinks better of it. He owes the Red God three lives for the three he should have lost that day and offers to spend them on whomever she names; she wastes the first on a guard she points at out of pure frustration, spends the second deliberately on the Tickler, and burns the third — out of patience with his rules rather than any plan — on Jaqen H'ghar himself. He poisons the garrison's wine at Harrenhal to make good on the debt, sees her and Gendry and Hot Pie out through the chaos, and leaves her an iron coin, a password, and an open door in Braavos if she ever wants to stop being Arya Stark."],
    5: ["The same face, or a face wearing his name, running the House of Black and White in Braavos when Arya finally crosses the sea to collect on his old offer. He has her wash corpses and lie about who she is until she can do it without flinching, tests her with poisoned water and borrowed identities, and sets the Waif to needle her at every turn. Permitted at last to kill a corrupt merchant who has been selling doomed cargo insurance, she does the job cleanly — and then, without permission, uses a stolen face to hunt down a man from her own list for reasons that have nothing to do with the Many-Faced God. The House does not forgive for free, and by the end of the season Arya is paying for it in the dark, robbed of her sight on its own front steps."],
  },

  "The Waif": {
    5: ["An acolyte of the House of Black and White already, and cold toward the new girl in exactly the way the House requires. She turns up in Arya's training wearing other people's lives — a beggar, a highborn lady, whoever the exercise calls for — to see whether Arya can spot the same person underneath, and rarely lets her pass the test without a bruise for the trouble. When Arya starts keeping a face of her own and a list the House never approved, the Waif is the one person in the building who seems to notice, and to disapprove, well before Jaqen ever says a word about it."],
    6: ["Sent to finish what blindness started, and drills Arya through the streets of Braavos with a wooden staff, knocking her down again and again until the excuse stops working. When Arya's mercy toward a Braavosi actress costs her Jaqen's patience for good — given one job, she gave the woman a warning instead — the Waif is handed the contract on Arya herself, and this time it isn't a training exercise. She stabs her in the street, hunts her wounded through the city, and kills the actress who sheltered her along the way, cornering Arya at last with nothing between them but a dark room and a candle. She has never once underestimated Arya's blade; what she doesn't account for is a girl who has spent a season learning to fight without her eyes."],
  },

  "Xaro Xhoan Daxos": {
    2: ["A Qartheen merchant prince and one of the Thirteen who rule the city, and the first person there to treat Daenerys like an asset worth acquiring rather than a curiosity to gawk at. He houses her exhausted khalasar, proposes marriage, and promises ships and an army if she'll grant him a claim on her dragons, all delivered with the practiced warmth of a man who has made this offer before. What he's actually doing, alongside the warlock Pyat Pree, is arranging a massacre of his fellow Thirteen and the theft of her dragons so he can crown himself king of Qarth unopposed. She takes the dragons back from the House of the Undying, walks into his mansion, and finds the great vault he's always boasting about is as empty as his promises — so she seals him inside it with Doreah and sails on with his ships instead."],
  },

  "The High Sparrow": {
    5: ["A barefoot ascetic found scrubbing latrines outside the Great Sept, more interested in bread for the poor than gold for himself, which is exactly what makes Cersei think she can use him. She has him named High Septon and helps him rearm the Faith Militant to threaten the Tyrells, never considering that a man with no appetites is a man with nothing to hold over him. He arrests Loras for his indiscretions and Margaery for lying to cover them, and when Cersei's own sins come out through her cousin Lancel's confession, he locks her in a cell too. By the season's end she is shaved, stripped and walked barefoot through King's Landing while his septas ring bells and the city spits on her."],
    6: ["He keeps Cersei's trial suspended rather than settled, which suits him better than a verdict would — a queen kept waiting is a queen kept humble. He works on Tommen instead of her, courting the boy king with sermons about faith and crown until Tommen bans trial by combat outright, closing the one door Cersei might have escaped through. He plans a public reckoning at the Sept of Baelor for Cersei, Loras and Margaery together, certain the day belongs to him. Cersei does not attend. The Sept goes up in green fire under the city he thought he had tamed, and he is still inside it when it does."],
  },

  "Septa Unella": {
    5: ["The High Sparrow's most devoted jailer, a woman who has clearly been waiting her whole life for sinners worth this much attention. Once Cersei is taken into the Faith's custody, Unella is the one at her cell door morning after morning, demanding a confession with the patience of someone who knows time is on her side. She gets one eventually, though not the whole of it, and it earns Cersei only a walk through the streets rather than freedom. Unella walks just behind her the entire way, ringing a bell and chanting the word she has been saying for weeks: shame, shame, shame."],
    6: ["Cersei does not forget who rang the bell. Once the crown is hers outright, Unella is dragged into a cell of her own and chained to a chair across from the queen she used to break down, and Cersei returns every question with interest — was it good, does the Mother judge her, does she still believe confession earns mercy. She leaves Unella with a mocking echo of her own catchphrase and the Mountain standing silent in the corner, and walks out. Nobody sees Unella again, and nobody left in the Red Keep is inclined to ask."],
  },

  "Gregor Clegane": {
    1: ["Ser Gregor Clegane, called the Mountain, enters the Hand's tourney a favorite and leaves it in a rage — unhorsed by Ser Loras Tyrell, who has the sense to ride a mare in heat against Gregor's stallion. He beheads his own horse on the spot and comes for Loras on foot with a longsword, and it is his younger brother Sandor who steps between them and stops a killing in front of the whole city. Gregor walks off without a word, which is somehow worse than if he had said something. His reputation as the worst man in Westeros arrives well before he does anything else to earn it."],
    4: ["Named Cersei's champion for Tyrion's trial by combat against Prince Oberyn of Dorne, and it should be no contest — a mountain of a man against one lithe Dornishman with a spear. Oberyn is faster and lands blow after blow, and makes the mistake of stopping to make Gregor confess to raping and murdering his sister Elia and her children instead of finishing him. Gregor answers by crushing Oberyn's skull with his bare hands in front of the entire court, but not before Oberyn's poisoned spear has already opened him up. He is carried off dying, and disappears into Qyburn's care to be worked on by methods nobody in the Red Keep wants described to them."],
    6: ["He returns as something else entirely — a hulking, silent shape in full plate who never removes his helm and never speaks, standing at Cersei's shoulder wherever she goes. When the High Sparrow sends a small sparrow to summon her to the Sept, the thing that was Gregor Clegane picks the man up and throws him out of a tower window without breaking stride. Whatever Qyburn did to him, it left a bodyguard who does not tire, does not bleed the way a man should, and does not need persuading to do anything Cersei asks of him."],
    7: ["Qyburn presents him to the small council as Ser Robert Strong, freshly sworn to the Kingsguard, and nobody at that table asks the obvious question twice. At the Dragonpit parley he stands over Cersei in full armor while his brother Sandor stares him down across the yard, close enough to speak to him, and gets nothing back — not recognition, not anger, nothing a living man would give another. Beric Dondarrion holds a flaming sword near him to test something, and whatever it proves, it is not comforting to anyone who remembers what Gregor used to be."],
    8: ["The Battle of King's Landing finally gives Sandor the fight he has wanted since the first time he ever looked at his brother's face — armies and dragons forgotten the moment he spots him in the burning city. He puts sword after sword into Gregor and gets nothing back for it; the thing wearing his brother's face barely registers the wounds, and Sandor accepts a plain fact he has spent seasons dodging — the Mountain other men could hurt died a long time ago. Their fight carries both of them up through a collapsing tower and out into the fire raging beneath the Red Keep, and neither one comes back down."],
  },

  "Meryn Trant": {
    1: ["A Kingsguard under Robert and then Joffrey, and one of the few in white cloaks who never bothers dressing up his cruelty as duty. After Ned Stark is arrested he is sent with a squad of gold cloaks to seize Arya from her dancing lessons, and it is his blade that ends the standoff with Syrio Forel, who holds the stairwell with nothing but a wooden practice sword long enough for her to run. He is in the crowd at Baelor's steps when Ned is executed, one white cloak among several, doing the job the office requires without needing to be told twice."],
    2: ["Joffrey finds a use for him beyond standing guard: when Robb Stark wins another battle in the field, Joffrey has Meryn Trant beat Sansa for her brother's victories, and he does it in open court without hesitation, tearing at her clothes on command until Tyrion arrives to put a stop to it. He carries out the order the way he carries out every order from Joffrey, with no visible feeling about it either way."],
    4: ["Still in white at Joffrey's wedding to Margaery, positioned near the high table when the king starts choking on poisoned wine, and he does exactly as much to help as every other Kingsguard in the room, which is nothing useful. He stays on with the household after the crown passes to Tommen, one more sword nobody thinks to reassign."],
    5: ["Sent to Braavos on the Small Council's business, where he spends his evenings at a theater that turns out to be a front for something much worse, buying the attention of the girls who work there. One of the newer girls, quiet and good with a blade, wins his trust over several visits before he ever learns her name is Arya Stark. She kills him in a back room, taking her time about it, and leaves the theater a small, precise settlement of a debt several seasons old."],
  },

  "Ilyn Payne": {
    1: ["The King's Justice, and a man who had his tongue torn out by order of a Mad King long dead, so whatever he thinks about the work he does, nobody will ever hear it. He stands silent at official functions, a hollow-cheeked reminder of what the crown can do to a body, and when Ned Stark is dragged to the steps of Baelor's Sept to confess treason, it is Ilyn Payne holding the greatsword. Promises of mercy from Varys and Cersei turn out to be worth nothing, the crowd is a crowd, and it is his blade that comes down, adding a Stark to the very long list of things he has been ordered to do without comment."],
    2: ["Riding with a Lannister patrol into the Riverlands, one sword among many hunting whoever the crown wants hunted, and Arya spots him across a camp before he ever notices her — a face she has spent a season trying to forget, wearing the same iron quiet he always wears. She goes still and says nothing, because saying anything near Ilyn Payne has only ever gone one way for people she loves. He rides on without knowing how close he came to being recognized, or how far up somebody's list his name already sits."],
  },

  "The Night King": {
    4: ["Introduced without a word, because he will never need one — the White Walkers carry an infant taken from Craster's sons out onto the ice, lay him on a stone altar, and a tall pale figure with eyes like winter touches the boy's face. The crying stops, the baby's eyes turn blue, and he sits up a different thing entirely. It is the first time the show explains where the Others actually come from and who is making them, and the answer is this one figure, patient and wordless, standing at the head of everything cold north of the Wall."],
    5: ["At Hardhome he watches an entire wildling refuge collapse into slaughter and barely needs to lift a blade himself until the very end, when Jon Snow finally sees him in the open and the two of them meet eyes across a burning shore. When the fighting is over and the wildling dead lie scattered on the ice, he raises his arms, unhurried, and every corpse on that beach stands back up at once and falls in behind him. Jon and what's left of the living escape by boat. The Night King does not chase. He does not need to."],
    6: ["Bran's visions finally show where he came from — the Children of the Forest, centuries ago, driving a dragonglass blade into a captive man's chest to build a weapon against the First Men, and getting something they could never fully control instead. In the present he leads his army straight to the cave behind the Wall where Bran and Meera are hiding, tears through the last of Bran's protectors to reach him, and grabs his arm out in the snow. The mark he leaves behind is not a wound so much as an invitation, and everything the Wall was ever supposed to keep out now has a way in."],
    7: ["He rides north to the lake beyond Eastwatch and answers Jon Snow's rescue party the only way he knows how — with an ice spear thrown clean through Viserion's neck, dropping a live dragon into black water in front of everyone who came to stop him. Days later the dragon rises again from the lake, reborn blue-eyed and undead in his service, and he flies it to the Wall himself and breathes it open at Eastwatch, walking his army through the gap under a sky on fire. Seven Kingdoms' worth of magic built to keep him out amounts to a wall with a hole in it now."],
    8: ["He marches the dead south to Winterfell and lets the living exhaust themselves throwing everything they have at his army first, arrows and fire and cavalry charges, before he ever enters the fight himself. When he finally does, he goes straight for Bran, unbothered by dragons or by Jon Snow's blade, riding a reanimated Viserion into the godswood as if the whole battle has been a formality until this moment. He never sees Arya Stark coming. She puts a dagger into him instead of Bran, and everything he ever made — every corpse he ever raised — goes down with him in the same instant, dust and stillness where an army stood a second before."],
  },

  "Lyanna Mormont": {
    6: ["Not yet a teenager, and already Lady of Bear Island, and the only northern lord who does not blink when Jon Snow and Sansa Stark come asking for swords. She hears Jon out, tells him Bear Island's men are needed at home, and sends him off with sixty-two of them anyway — more than he expected, less than he wanted. After the Boltons fall she stands up in a hall full of grown men who waited to see who would win before picking a side, and shames every one of them into kneeling; Jon becomes King in the North because a child spoke first."],
    7: ["Still the smallest voice in the room and still the one nobody interrupts twice. She sits on Sansa's council as Winterfell braces for winter, wary of promises made by southern queens and impatient with anyone who treats her age as a reason to leave. When word comes that Jon has bent the knee to Daenerys Targaryen in the south, she says what half the hall is thinking — that he was their king, crowned by their own lords, and has given it away without asking them — before the argument moves past her and the North holds together regardless."],
    8: ["Lady of Bear Island in a castle full of strangers, unimpressed by dragons, Dothraki, or Tormund Giantsbane's stories about the far north, and as blunt as ever about what Bear Island can still spare for the fight. In the dark of the Long Night a wight giant sweeps her off her feet, and dying in its fist she drives a dragonglass blade through its eye — a kill nobody twice her size manages that night, and the reason Bear Island's name is said with something more than pity afterward."],
  },

  "Randyll Tarly": {
    6: ["Lord of Horn Hill, and the reason Samwell Tarly joined the Night's Watch rather than inherit anything. He receives his son's uninvited family — a wildling mother and a baby that is not his blood — at his own dinner table and spends the meal making sure everyone present understands exactly how little he thinks of Sam: no warrior, no hunter, unfit to carry Heartsbane or the family name. He orders Gilly sent to the kitchens and Sam back to the Wall alone; Sam answers by walking out that night with Gilly, the baby, and his family's Valyrian steel sword under his arm."],
    7: ["When Highgarden falls, House Tarly is the reason it falls easily — Randyll reads the field, abandons the Tyrell cause, and bends the knee to Cersei Lannister rather than lose everything backing a dying house. He leads the column that guts the Reach's granaries and rides with the army Daenerys catches in the open at the Loot Train. Taken prisoner afterward and offered the mercy of bending the knee a second time, he refuses outright — pride and contempt for a queen he considers little better than a foreign invader outlast his instinct for survival — and Daenerys burns him for it."],
  },

  "Dickon Tarly": {
    6: ["The son his father actually wanted, home on leave and polite about it, letting Randyll's insults to Sam land without adding any of his own. He says little at the dinner table beyond civil nothings to Gilly and mild agreement with whatever his father decides, comfortable in a house built entirely around his approval. It is a small, unremarkable role next to Sam's — which is rather the point: everything comes easily to Dickon that never came easily to his brother, and he has never once had to notice."],
    7: ["Fighting under his father's banner once Horn Hill turns its coat to Cersei, present for the sack of Highgarden and taken prisoner with him when Daenerys's dragon catches the Lannister column on the road home. Given the chance to live if he kneels, he starts to take it — offers his sword, tries to save them both — until his father calls it cowardice to his face. Dickon takes the offer back rather than wear that word, and burns beside Randyll moments later, dutiful to a fault and loyal to a man who was never once loyal to him."],
  },

  "Robin Arryn": {
    1: ["Lord of the Eyrie at somewhere around six years old, still at his mother's breast and prone to screaming fits the whole Vale is trained to indulge. His idea of justice, learned entirely from Lysa, is the Moon Door: when Catelyn Stark brings Tyrion Lannister to the Eyrie for trial, Robin spends the proceedings cheerfully asking whether the accused can fly yet. Tyrion talks and fights his way out of falling; not everyone who visits the Eyrie that year is so lucky."],
    4: ["Older, marginally, but no less sheltered — until Littlefinger arrives with a niece calling herself Alayne Stone and a mother who cannot stop watching the two of them together. Robin likes the new girl more than Lysa would prefer, and it costs Lysa exactly the composure she has left: a scene in the high hall, a threat against Sansa, and then Littlefinger's hand instead of a champion's putting someone through the Moon Door. Robin is left an orphan and Lord of the Vale in truth this time, with the man who killed his mother now the only adult he has left to trust."],
    6: ["Nearly grown and still hopeless with a bow, which Littlefinger — newly returned and newly indispensable — takes as an excuse to stand very close behind him and remind him whose side he should be on. Sansa arrives at the Eyrie asking for the Knights of the Vale, and it is Robin's word alongside Lord Royce's that sends them; the same boy who once wanted to watch Tyrion fly grows up just enough to help save Jon Snow's army at the last possible moment at the Battle of the Bastards."],
    8: ["A lord among lords now, one face in the crowd of Westeros's surviving great houses summoned to the Dragonpit to decide who rules them next. He has no lines that make it into the history books, but he is there — grown up, Lord of the Eyrie in more than name, watching the Seven Kingdoms get redrawn by men who once needed his word to move an army."],
  },

  "Lysa Arryn": {
    1: ["Catelyn's sister, Jon Arryn's widow, and the reason half the plot is set in motion: it is her letter, written at Littlefinger's urging, that names the Lannisters for her husband's murder and sends Ned Stark looking in the wrong dangerous direction. At the Eyrie she is queen of her own small, locked kingdom — hostile to visitors, controlling of her son to a degree that unsettles even Catelyn, and quick to put Tyrion Lannister on trial for a killing he did not do. She will not send Vale swords south for anyone's war, and no argument moves her on it."],
    4: ["Married to Littlefinger at last, and terrified of losing him the moment she has him — a fear that only sharpens when he arrives at the Eyrie with Sansa Stark in tow, calling her his niece and treating her with a tenderness Lysa has spent years waiting for herself. She corners Sansa at the Moon Door ready to throw her through it, certain of an affair that exists mostly in her own dread. Littlefinger talks her back from the edge with the gentlest voice she has heard from him in years, and then puts her through the door himself, telling her — accurately, for once — that he has only ever loved one woman, and it was never her."],
  },

  "Jojen Reed": {
    3: ["A boy from the Neck who sees things before they happen and has spent his whole life learning to live with that — greensighted, solemn beyond his years, and certain that Bran Stark is not merely hurt but meant for something the rest of the world has no word for yet. He and his sister Meera find Bran's party north of the Wall's shadow and simply attach themselves to it; Jojen does not ask permission because his visions have already told him he does not need it. He pushes Bran to stop mourning his legs and start learning what his dreams are for, however unwelcome the lesson."],
    4: ["North with Bran through wolf woods, a burned mill, and Craster's Keep's mutineers, walking toward a vision he has had for longer than anyone around him realizes: himself, at the door of a cave beneath a weirwood tree, not walking back out. When the dead come for them within sight of that door, he does not run — he has already spent a season making his peace with this exact moment, and he holds the line long enough for Meera and Bran to reach the tree without him. He dies where his greensight always told him he would, which does not make it easier for the people who loved him, only easier for him."],
  },

  "Meera Reed": {
    3: ["Jojen's older sister and the practical half of the partnership, a crannogwoman who fights with a net and a three-pronged frog spear and treats her brother's green dreams as a compass rather than a gospel. She and Jojen find Bran's party in the haunted forest, already looking for him before he knows he needs finding, and fall in with the group heading toward the Wall. At Queenscrown, she puts a wildling raider down in the dark with a spear-throw that leaves no doubt about what she is for — not company, protection."],
    4: ["Craster's Keep goes from unpleasant to lethal once Karl Tanner's mutineers get their hands on the group, and Meera is the one they corner — Hodor breaks his ropes and the moment before he starts killing men is the moment she understands what Bran can do with him. North at the cave under the weirwood, wights come up out of the snow in a swarm and she fights at Leaf's shoulder with fire and obsidian, but it is Jojen who does not get up afterward. She carries her brother's death into the cave along with Bran, and neither of them says much once they are inside."],
    6: ["Years pass inside the cave watching Bran vanish further into his visions while she keeps the fire going and waits for a purpose that keeps not arriving. When the Night King's army finds the weirwood, purpose arrives all at once: the Three-Eyed Raven and Summer and Leaf all die buying seconds, Hodor holds a door that cannot be held forever, and Meera is the one left to drag Bran through the snow on a sled, alone, half-frozen, with the dead somewhere behind her. She does not stop pulling until a rider she has never met finds them and does not ask permission before taking over."],
    7: ["The long drag ends at Winterfell, and so, not long after, does Meera's part in Bran's story. She delivers him through the gate half-dead and gets Maester's care instead of gratitude, and when she finally says what she has been carrying since the cave — that she gave up everything, that Jojen is dead, and that the boy in front of her is barely there to hear any of it — Bran's answer confirms exactly what she feared before she leaves for Greywater Watch, to go home to a family that still knows her name."],
  },

  "Talisa Stark": {
    2: ["A healer from Volantis working a field hospital in the Riverlands, unimpressed by lords and even less impressed by a king who has never had to saw off a man's leg himself. Robb Stark keeps finding reasons to visit her tent, and she keeps needling him about the war he is winning and the boys he is spending to win it. By the time his army marches on Oxcross the needling has turned into something else, and a king already promised to a Frey bride starts making choices that have nothing to do with strategy."],
    3: ["Married in secret and introduced to Robb's mother as a fact rather than a request, she becomes Queen in the North without much interest in the title itself. She rides with the army now, pregnant, and is the loudest voice arguing that executing Karstark for murdering two hostages will cost more than it settles — Robb overrules her and loses half his northern host anyway, so she turns out to be right and it changes nothing. At the Twins she comes as a guest to a wedding feast and does not leave it — stabbed through the belly along with the child she is carrying, in a hall her husband walked into trusting an old law to keep them safe."],
  },

  "Rickard Karstark": {
    2: ["One of Robb's greatest strengths on paper, the lord who brings the largest northern host to the war and sits close at every council that matters. His son stands guard over Jaime Lannister at Riverrun as a courtesy nobody thinks twice about, until Jaime strangles the boy with his own chain during a bungled escape attempt. Karstark buries a son and keeps fighting, but the grief does not go anywhere; it just waits for a second one to land on top of it."],
    3: ["By the time Catelyn frees Jaime Lannister without her son's leave, Karstark has already lost two sons to the war and has nothing left to trade for patience. He murders two Lannister boys being held hostage at Riverrun, cousins of Jaime's, killing them in their cells as payment nobody asked him to collect. Robb tries him for murder in front of the whole camp and executes him himself rather than pretend the law bends for grief, and half the Karstark host rides home in disgust the same day — the last northern lords Robb can afford to lose, gone because he would not look away from what one of them did."],
  },

  "Rickon Stark": {
    1: ["The youngest Stark, still small enough to be underfoot and mostly kept there, trailing a black direwolf pup too wild for anyone else to handle. Winterfell empties out around him one Stark at a time — his father south to the King's justice, his sisters after him, his older brothers to their own occupations — and Rickon is left in the nursery end of the castle, too young yet to be given a task or an explanation."],
    2: ["Theon Greyjoy's men come for Winterfell and Rickon is hidden in the crypts among the stone lords with his brother, too young to be calm about it and too angry to be quiet. Osha keeps a hand on him more than anyone else can manage; when the castle burns he watches it from the trees with the rest of the household that got out in time, and leaves the only home he has known walking north with his brother, a stableboy, a wildling woman, and two direwolves for company."],
    6: ["Years off the map end with Rickon delivered to Winterfell in chains, a gift from the Umbers to Ramsay Bolton along with what is left of Shaggydog. He spends his final scenes first as a hostage, then as bait, released into an open field during the battle for Winterfell with nothing but a head start and an order to run for his brother's line. He runs straight, the way a panicking boy would, and Ramsay puts an arrow in him a few strides short of Jon's outstretched arms — close enough that Jon has to watch it happen."],
  },

  "Hodor": {
    1: ["A servant of enormous size and few words — one word, in fact, said in every register from cheerful to frightened but never varied — who carries things and people around Winterfell without complaint. When Bran falls and loses the use of his legs, Hodor becomes his legs instead, hauling him through the castle strapped into a wicker frame on his back, patient with a boy who has every reason to be miserable company and mostly isn't, because of him."],
    2: ["When Theon takes the castle, Hodor is one of the few certainties left — he carries Bran into the crypts and then out of them, and out of Winterfell entirely, north into open country with no more plan than staying alive and staying together. He does not understand the politics that burned the castle down and does not need to; he understands that the boy on his back needs carrying, and keeps walking."],
    3: ["Somewhere on the road north Bran slips into his head without meaning to, and for the first time Hodor is not entirely himself — he goes rigid and starts saying his own name like it is being pulled out of him, which frightens everyone traveling with him more than it frightens Hodor, who does not remember it afterward. He keeps carrying Bran anyway, unbothered by a thing he cannot explain and was never going to be asked to understand."],
    4: ["Tied up and threatened at Craster's Keep, Hodor is the one Bran reaches for when the situation stops being survivable any other way, and the size that has only ever been used for carrying gets used, briefly and thoroughly, for killing. At the cave under the weirwood he fights the dead alongside Summer and Meera with whatever comes to hand, still saying only his own name through all of it, the one word doing service as war cry and complaint and everything in between."],
    6: ["The end of it arrives out of order: as the Night King's dead reach the cave, Bran reaches backward through Hodor's whole life at the same moment he needs him most, and in a vision decades removed a boy named Wylis collapses at Winterfell, repeating an order that has not been given to him yet until it wears down into a single word. In the present, Hodor holds a door against the dead so Meera can drag Bran clear, holds it long after holding it stops being survivable, and dies keeping it shut. The name he has answered to his whole life turns out to have been assigned to him, years in advance, by the exact moment that kills him."],
  },

  "Osha": {
    1: ["A wildling caught on the wrong side of the Wall with a band of raiders, marched into Winterfell as a captive and kept alive by putting her to work in the kitchens rather than a cell. She tells anyone who will listen what she saw before she was caught — the dead walking, and worse than dead — and gets mostly disbelief for her trouble, filed away as a wildling's tall tale by people who have never had a reason to need it to be true."],
    2: ["When Theon takes Winterfell, Osha is the one who keeps her head — she hides Bran and Rickon and the direwolves in the crypts, and when hiding stops being enough she trades a night with one of Theon's men for the chance to get the boys out unnoticed. She has no loyalty to the Starks so much as a working contempt for anyone who lets children get killed over politics, and that turns out to be loyalty enough to get Bran and Rickon out of a burning castle alive."],
    3: ["North with Bran's party, Osha has no patience for Jojen Reed's green dreams and even less for the idea of taking two highborn boys beyond the Wall to chase them. She has kept Bran and Rickon alive on practical terms since Winterfell fell, and practical terms say the youngest Stark left does not follow his brother into wherever this vision is leading. She takes Rickon and Shaggydog and turns off the road toward Last Hearth instead, choosing the boy she can still protect over the one who has stopped needing her kind of protecting."],
    6: ["Years of hiding end with Osha and Rickon handed over to Ramsay Bolton by the Umbers as proof of loyalty, Shaggydog's head included as a bonus nobody asked for. She tries the trick that got Bran and Rickon out of Winterfell once before, working a blade loose to use on a man who trusts her enough to get close — but Ramsay knows exactly what kind of trick it is, names it before she can finish it, and kills her with her own knife while she is pinned in place. She dies having spent her entire war trying to keep one boy alive, with no way left to stop him from being handed to the person who kills him next."],
  },

  "Syrio Forel": {
    1: ["A Braavosi dancing master hired to make a highborn girl graceful and gets instead a pupil who wants to fight. He teaches Arya Stark with a broom handle and a string of maxims — water dancing, not the iron kind, and always the same question, 'what do we say to the god of death.' When the gold cloaks come for Arya in the tower and find the doors barred, he sends her running and turns to meet Meryn Trant and four armed men holding nothing but a wooden sword. The scene cuts before it ends. He is not seen again, and the show never tells the audience whether the First Sword of Braavos wins that fight or loses it."],
  },

  "Old Nan": {
    1: ["Winterfell's oldest resident, blind and shrunken and older than anyone can say for certain, kept on now less as a nurse than as a teller of stories nobody quite believes. She sits with Bran after the fall that leaves him crippled and tells him the tale he is too young for — the Long Night, and the Others who came down out of the dark eight thousand years ago with blue eyes and cold hands, and the Night's King who betrayed the Watch for love of one of them. She means it as a bedtime story, something to pass a sickroom hour. Nobody in earshot, least of all her, seems to notice how much of it is about to come true."],
  },

  "Hot Pie": {
    1: ["A baker's boy conscripted for the Wall along with dozens of King's Landing's least martial residents, swept up by Yoren after Ned Stark's execution leaves half the city looking for somewhere else to be. He is soft, hungry, and not remotely built for soldiering, and he says so, loudly and often, to a fellow recruit called Arry who is keeping a rather bigger secret than he is. The company files out through the gates and onto the kingsroad north, Hot Pie grumbling about his feet the whole way, with no idea that the boy walking beside him is a girl, a Stark, and the reason Ser Meryn Trant keeps asking questions at every crossing."],
    2: ["North on the kingsroad with Yoren's company, more useful with a rolling pin than a sword and increasingly aware of it. When Lannister soldiers cut the party down and kill Yoren, he survives only by being unremarkable enough to march to Harrenhal as a captive rather than a corpse, and spends weeks in Lord Tywin's kitchens turning out pigeon pie good enough to catch the Hand's own notice. He helps Arya and Gendry slip away when the chance finally comes, but when the three of them reach a crossroads inn on the way south he decides he has had enough of running. He stays behind to knead dough for the innkeeper, and the road goes on without him."],
    6: ["Still at the crossroads inn years later, older, stouter, and evidently a competent baker, turning out loaves shaped like direwolves for reasons the show leaves charmingly unexplained. Brienne and Podrick stop there hunting for the Blackfish and find him instead, and he places Arya's old traveling name easily enough to tell them she was alive the last he heard, bound across the sea for Braavos. It is a small scene and a small kindness — a boy who chose bread over swords passing along the one piece of information that matters to the people asking, then turning back to his oven as they ride on without him."],
  },

  };

  Object.keys(ADD).forEach(function (name) {
    var cur = WIKI_EXTRA[name];
    if (Array.isArray(cur)) cur = { paras: cur };   /* the legacy plain-array shape */
    if (!cur) cur = {};
    cur.seasons = Object.assign(cur.seasons || {}, ADD[name]);
    WIKI_EXTRA[name] = cur;
  });
})();
